1
00:00:37,496 --> 00:00:41,500
[MUSIC CHATTER
PLAYING ON RADIO]

2
00:00:50,468 --> 00:00:54,472
MAN 1 [ON RADIO]:
<i>...clearly, I've never been there, but...</i>

3
00:00:56,474 --> 00:01:00,561
MAN 2: ...<i>usually the situation,
obviously a major malfunction.</i>

4
00:01:00,728 --> 00:01:03,481
[PEOPLE CHATTERING
IN FOREIGN LANGUAGES ON RADIO]

5
00:01:14,784 --> 00:01:17,036
MAN 3:
<i>...whether or not their president is a crook.</i>

6
00:01:17,203 --> 00:01:18,496
MAN 4:
<i>Well, I'm not a crook.</i>

7
00:01:18,663 --> 00:01:22,500
MAN 5:
<i>...small step for man, one giant leap for...</i>

8
00:01:22,667 --> 00:01:26,837
MAN 6: <i>Robert Kennedy was shot
in that ballroom.</i>

9
00:01:28,339 --> 00:01:31,008
MAN 7:
<i>Thank God almighty, we are free at last.</i>

10
00:01:31,175 --> 00:01:33,803
MAN 8:
<i>A sniper has fired at President Kennedy.</i>

11
00:01:41,185 --> 00:01:42,520
MAN 9:
<i>...the Communist party...</i>

12
00:01:42,687 --> 00:01:44,939
<i>...or have you ever been
a member of the Communist party?</i>

13
00:01:45,106 --> 00:01:49,360
MAN 10:
<i>Old soldiers never die. They just...</i>

14
00:01:49,527 --> 00:01:52,113
MAN 11:
<i>Hi-yo, Silver!</i>

15
00:01:53,781 --> 00:01:56,617
MAN 12:
<i>...something never before experienced...</i>

16
00:01:56,784 --> 00:02:01,205
MAN 13:
<i>...1941, a date which will live...</i>

17
00:02:05,543 --> 00:02:09,922
MAN 14: <i>And we continue this final edition
of our Maxwell House Good News of 1939.</i>

18
00:02:10,089 --> 00:02:12,216
MAN 15: <i>Good evening,
Mr. and Mrs. North America...</i>

19
00:02:12,383 --> 00:02:14,760
<i>...and all the ships at sea.
Let's go to press.</i>

20
00:02:18,973 --> 00:02:23,394
[MUSIC AND CHATTER FADING]

21
00:03:32,296 --> 00:03:36,467
GIRL:
<i>CQ, this is W-9 GFO.</i>

22
00:03:37,968 --> 00:03:42,431
CQ, this is W-9 GFO here. Come back?

23
00:03:44,016 --> 00:03:45,434
CQ.

24
00:03:45,601 --> 00:03:50,106
CQ, this is W-9 GFO here.

25
00:03:50,272 --> 00:03:51,941
Come back?

26
00:03:52,483 --> 00:03:53,943
[KNOCKING]

27
00:03:54,110 --> 00:03:55,277
[DOOR OPENS]

28
00:03:55,444 --> 00:04:00,449
CQ, CQ, this is W-9 GFO.
Is anybody out there?

29
00:04:00,616 --> 00:04:01,909
Not getting anything.

30
00:04:02,076 --> 00:04:03,661
Small moves, Ellie.

31
00:04:05,121 --> 00:04:06,497
Small moves.

32
00:04:08,999 --> 00:04:13,504
CQ, this is W-9 GFO here. Come back?

33
00:04:13,671 --> 00:04:17,675
MAN:
<i>Copy, W-9 GFO. K-4 WLD here.</i>

34
00:04:17,842 --> 00:04:21,095
- What do I say?
- Just be yourself.

35
00:04:21,554 --> 00:04:25,766
- Where are you, K-4 WLD? Come back?
<i>- Pensacola. Over.</i>

36
00:04:25,933 --> 00:04:27,518
Pensacola, where's Pensacola?

37
00:04:27,685 --> 00:04:29,687
I'll give you a hint.

38
00:04:29,854 --> 00:04:31,147
Orange juice.

39
00:04:33,858 --> 00:04:37,695
Copy that, K-4 WLD.
How's the weather down there in Florida?

40
00:04:38,988 --> 00:04:41,240
TED:
Pensacola, Florida.

41
00:04:41,407 --> 00:04:44,076
I have to tell you, Sparks...

42
00:04:44,243 --> 00:04:47,746
...one thousand, one hundred
and sixteen miles.

43
00:04:47,913 --> 00:04:51,375
Pretty good. That's the farthest yet.

44
00:05:03,762 --> 00:05:07,057
ELLIE: Can we hear all the way to New York?
TED: Sure.

45
00:05:08,434 --> 00:05:12,396
ELLIE: Can we hear all the way to California?
TED: I don't see why not.

46
00:05:13,147 --> 00:05:16,859
ELLIE: Can we hear all the way to Alaska?
TED: Yeah, on a really clear day.

47
00:05:17,067 --> 00:05:19,570
ELLIE:
Can we hear all the way to China?

48
00:05:20,112 --> 00:05:22,156
TED:
On a really, really clear day.

49
00:05:22,323 --> 00:05:23,741
[CHUCKLING]

50
00:05:24,450 --> 00:05:27,077
ELLIE:
Could we talk to the moon?

51
00:05:27,244 --> 00:05:30,080
TED: Well, if it's a big enough radio,
I don't see why not.

52
00:05:30,748 --> 00:05:32,750
ELLIE:
Could we talk to Jupiter?

53
00:05:32,958 --> 00:05:35,753
Or what's the one after that?

54
00:05:35,920 --> 00:05:37,755
Don't tell me.

55
00:05:39,590 --> 00:05:41,258
TED:
I'll give you a hint.

56
00:05:41,425 --> 00:05:42,593
Hula-Hoops.

57
00:05:42,801 --> 00:05:46,096
- Saturn. Could we talk to Saturn?
- Mm-hm.

58
00:05:50,684 --> 00:05:53,687
Dad, could we talk to Mom?

59
00:05:58,776 --> 00:06:01,862
I don't think even the biggest radio
could reach that far.

60
00:06:06,992 --> 00:06:09,286
All right, no more stalling.

61
00:06:10,829 --> 00:06:12,289
Okay.

62
00:06:13,874 --> 00:06:15,251
Pensacola.

63
00:06:22,049 --> 00:06:24,385
Oh, Ellie, that's a beauty.

64
00:06:25,886 --> 00:06:27,638
Now get some sleep.

65
00:06:35,646 --> 00:06:37,690
- Hey, Dad?
- Yeah?

66
00:06:37,856 --> 00:06:40,651
Do you think there's people
on other planets?

67
00:06:40,818 --> 00:06:42,486
I don't know, Sparks.

68
00:06:43,988 --> 00:06:45,990
But I guess I'd say...

69
00:06:46,156 --> 00:06:48,325
...if it is just us...

70
00:06:49,493 --> 00:06:51,829
...it seems like an awful waste of space.

71
00:07:08,846 --> 00:07:11,181
CQ, CQ.

72
00:07:11,348 --> 00:07:13,684
CQ, this is W-9 GFO.

73
00:07:13,892 --> 00:07:17,021
Repeat. This is W-9 GFO. Come back?

74
00:07:18,564 --> 00:07:21,942
CQ, this is W-9 GFO. Come back?

75
00:07:24,570 --> 00:07:27,031
I'm gonna need a bigger antenna.

76
00:07:53,599 --> 00:07:55,351
MAN:
What do you think, Doctor Arroway?

77
00:07:55,517 --> 00:07:57,519
Isn't she a beauty?

78
00:08:01,065 --> 00:08:02,191
It'll do.

79
00:08:02,399 --> 00:08:04,360
FISHER:
The village is five miles away.

80
00:08:04,568 --> 00:08:09,156
There's a general store in the cantina.
They can order anything from San Juan.

81
00:08:09,323 --> 00:08:10,908
ELLIE:
And when can I get some dish time?

82
00:08:11,075 --> 00:08:14,453
FISHER: Dr. Clark said you wouldn't
be able to wait. Your first shift is tonight.

83
00:08:14,620 --> 00:08:15,746
ELLIE:
All right.

84
00:09:00,958 --> 00:09:03,293
[STATIC BUZZING OVER RADIO]

85
00:09:10,884 --> 00:09:12,386
Go back.

86
00:09:13,804 --> 00:09:16,890
Try back a little bit.
You had something there.

87
00:09:17,057 --> 00:09:18,100
[WHIRRING ON RADIO]

88
00:09:18,267 --> 00:09:19,351
There.

89
00:09:19,518 --> 00:09:22,604
- Right there?
- You hear it? Right there, that's it.

90
00:09:22,771 --> 00:09:26,650
ELLIE: All right, 1221.46.
That's well within the I band.

91
00:09:26,817 --> 00:09:29,903
- Check off-axis.
- Uh-huh. On it.

92
00:09:30,988 --> 00:09:33,657
It's not in the neighborhood.

93
00:09:39,288 --> 00:09:40,956
Ah.

94
00:09:42,124 --> 00:09:46,003
Here it is. J-1741 plus 2748.

95
00:09:46,170 --> 00:09:49,840
Catalogued November 4, 1982. Pulsar.

96
00:09:50,674 --> 00:09:55,179
Well, I don't know, it must've been...
The glitch in the timing is what threw me.

97
00:09:55,345 --> 00:09:57,055
Must've been a starquake.

98
00:09:57,222 --> 00:09:58,265
[WHIRRING STOPS]

99
00:09:58,432 --> 00:10:02,269
I think it's great that you listen.
Most people don't do that anymore.

100
00:10:02,436 --> 00:10:05,314
It's just an old habit.
You know, it makes it feel more real.

101
00:10:05,481 --> 00:10:08,358
- Ellie.
- Kent. Kent Clark.

102
00:10:09,401 --> 00:10:10,527
Hey.

103
00:10:10,694 --> 00:10:14,990
They said you've been up at Owens Valley
working under Drumlin.

104
00:10:15,157 --> 00:10:16,742
How did you like him?

105
00:10:16,909 --> 00:10:18,202
[ELLIE SIGHS]

106
00:10:19,703 --> 00:10:24,041
That much? And about what I'd expect
after what he had to say about you.

107
00:10:24,208 --> 00:10:26,210
What was that?

108
00:10:26,376 --> 00:10:29,505
That you are brilliant, driven...

109
00:10:29,671 --> 00:10:32,674
...a major pain in the ass,
and obsessed with a field of study...

110
00:10:32,841 --> 00:10:36,011
...that he considers tantamount
to professional suicide.

111
00:10:36,929 --> 00:10:40,057
- Anything else?
- Those were the highlights.

112
00:10:40,224 --> 00:10:42,518
Davio, Dr. Vernon. Dr. Arroway.

113
00:10:42,684 --> 00:10:44,144
ELLIE: Hey. Hi there.
- How you doing?

114
00:10:44,311 --> 00:10:48,899
And I believe you've already met our
tireless research assistant, Mr. Fisher...

115
00:10:49,066 --> 00:10:51,860
- Here, I need you to sign...
- ...as well as his cologne.

116
00:10:52,027 --> 00:10:54,613
Brook's looking at the black hole
at the center of M-87.

117
00:10:54,780 --> 00:10:58,534
Eli's studying Markarian 541,
a major gamma-ray source.

118
00:10:58,700 --> 00:11:03,747
And Dr. Arroway will be spending her
precious telescope time listening for, um...

119
00:11:04,122 --> 00:11:06,124
Listening for...

120
00:11:06,291 --> 00:11:08,085
Little green men.

121
00:11:15,843 --> 00:11:19,429
One down, a couple billion to go.

122
00:11:22,182 --> 00:11:23,851
[SPEAKING IN SPANISH]

123
00:11:36,113 --> 00:11:38,073
Arecibo, right?

124
00:11:39,241 --> 00:11:40,409
Does it show?

125
00:11:41,034 --> 00:11:42,286
Yeah.

126
00:11:42,452 --> 00:11:44,746
- Cracker Jack?
- No, thanks.

127
00:11:44,913 --> 00:11:46,456
- Mind if I sit down?
- Sure.

128
00:11:48,125 --> 00:11:52,129
I hear the locals, they call it El Radar.

129
00:11:52,296 --> 00:11:55,132
Convinced it has
some dark military purpose.

130
00:11:55,299 --> 00:11:56,717
I think we're pretty harmless.

131
00:11:58,176 --> 00:11:59,303
Palmer Joss.

132
00:12:00,137 --> 00:12:02,598
- Ellie Arroway.
- Nice to meet you, Ellie.

133
00:12:03,807 --> 00:12:06,768
What are you studying up there?

134
00:12:08,437 --> 00:12:09,813
The usual.

135
00:12:09,980 --> 00:12:13,025
You know. Nebulae, quasars, pulsars,
stuff like that.

136
00:12:13,191 --> 00:12:15,777
What are you...? What are you writing?

137
00:12:15,944 --> 00:12:20,324
The usual. Nouns, adverbs,
adjective here and there.

138
00:12:21,658 --> 00:12:23,160
I work on a project called SETI.

139
00:12:23,952 --> 00:12:25,996
Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence?

140
00:12:26,663 --> 00:12:30,250
Wow. Man, that is out there.

141
00:12:31,043 --> 00:12:32,878
Wait, are you a student or something?

142
00:12:33,045 --> 00:12:36,340
Mm-mm. I'm a writer. I'm writing a book.

143
00:12:36,506 --> 00:12:41,178
Down here doing research about how
technology affects Third World cultures.

144
00:12:41,345 --> 00:12:44,181
I'm also looking for this guy
David Drumlin.

145
00:12:44,348 --> 00:12:48,810
He's the new head of the National Science
Foundation. I'm trying to get an interview.

146
00:12:49,686 --> 00:12:52,689
- I take it you know him?
- Mm-hm.

147
00:12:52,856 --> 00:12:54,816
You could say that.

148
00:12:55,651 --> 00:12:59,321
SETI, man. That's fringe.

149
00:12:59,488 --> 00:13:01,031
I've crossed paths with this guy.

150
00:13:01,198 --> 00:13:03,867
Something like that
must really chap his ass, huh?

151
00:13:06,828 --> 00:13:08,205
Compass.

152
00:13:09,873 --> 00:13:11,458
For you, EI.

153
00:13:16,046 --> 00:13:17,839
You better keep this.

154
00:13:18,715 --> 00:13:20,842
Might save your life someday.

155
00:13:25,013 --> 00:13:26,807
Will you have dinner with me tonight?

156
00:13:28,350 --> 00:13:29,685
I...

157
00:13:29,851 --> 00:13:33,480
I don't make a very good research subject.
I'm just not very quotable.

158
00:13:33,647 --> 00:13:37,401
No quotes. No quotes. Scout's honor.
Just a good meal, good company.

159
00:13:38,235 --> 00:13:40,070
I gotta go.

160
00:13:43,281 --> 00:13:45,409
But, uh...

161
00:13:48,704 --> 00:13:51,039
Drumlin's coming in this Tuesday.

162
00:13:59,423 --> 00:14:03,301
Now I remember why I took that desk job.

163
00:14:04,302 --> 00:14:06,847
- Dr. Drumlin, how are you?
- Dr. Drumlin.

164
00:14:07,806 --> 00:14:09,891
Glad you could make it, David.

165
00:14:10,350 --> 00:14:13,603
If I'd known it was gonna take
three planes, I might've reconsidered.

166
00:14:13,770 --> 00:14:15,147
How is that new office?

167
00:14:15,313 --> 00:14:19,901
I'm still settling in. They already wanna take
half my budget and give it to the Pentagon.

168
00:14:20,068 --> 00:14:22,946
Ellie. Still waiting for E.T. to call?

169
00:14:23,113 --> 00:14:27,117
- Well, I understand we're having a little...
- Good to see you too.

170
00:14:27,284 --> 00:14:28,285
Asshole.

171
00:14:28,452 --> 00:14:30,787
Uh-oh. Looks like
it's gonna be a long night.

172
00:14:30,954 --> 00:14:35,417
Science must first be accountable to the
people who are paying for it, the taxpayers.

173
00:14:35,584 --> 00:14:38,962
We need to stop wasting money
on pie-in-the-sky abstractions...

174
00:14:39,129 --> 00:14:42,090
...and start spending it on practical,
measurable ways...

175
00:14:42,257 --> 00:14:45,135
...to improve the lives of the people
who are footing the bill.

176
00:14:45,302 --> 00:14:48,430
Not unlike my L-band,
globular-clusters work.

177
00:14:48,597 --> 00:14:51,641
Wait, you're saying you wanna do away
with all pure research now?

178
00:14:51,808 --> 00:14:55,145
What's wrong with science being practical?
Even profitable?

179
00:14:55,353 --> 00:14:59,191
Nothing, as long as your motive
is the search for truth.

180
00:14:59,357 --> 00:15:02,486
Which is exactly what
the pursuit of science is.

181
00:15:04,029 --> 00:15:05,864
That's a rather interesting position...

182
00:15:06,031 --> 00:15:09,701
...coming from a man on a crusade
against the evils of technology...

183
00:15:09,868 --> 00:15:10,952
...Father Joss.

184
00:15:11,620 --> 00:15:13,997
PALMER:
I'm not against technology, doctor.

185
00:15:14,164 --> 00:15:18,418
I'm against the men who deify it
at the expense of human truth.

186
00:15:22,255 --> 00:15:25,467
Kent, I've been meaning to have
a word with you. Over here, please.

187
00:15:25,634 --> 00:15:27,511
KENT:
Yeah, I'll just...

188
00:15:31,515 --> 00:15:34,643
I think that hurt my chances
with that interview. What do you think?

189
00:15:35,268 --> 00:15:37,104
You're a priest?

190
00:15:38,605 --> 00:15:39,856
No, not really.

191
00:15:40,524 --> 00:15:43,610
I got my Masters in Divinity...

192
00:15:43,777 --> 00:15:47,364
...then dropped out of seminary to
do some secular humanitarian work...

193
00:15:47,531 --> 00:15:50,033
...coordinating efforts
for Third World churches.

194
00:15:50,617 --> 00:15:53,286
I couldn't live with
the whole celibacy thing.

195
00:15:55,664 --> 00:15:57,040
You could...

196
00:15:57,207 --> 00:15:59,793
You could call me a man of the cloth...

197
00:16:00,627 --> 00:16:02,212
...without the cloth.

198
00:16:05,549 --> 00:16:07,217
You wanna get out of here?

199
00:16:09,136 --> 00:16:12,639
All right, you see that sort of large,
W-shaped constellation right there?

200
00:16:12,806 --> 00:16:14,266
That's Cassiopeia.

201
00:16:14,432 --> 00:16:18,979
And Cassiopeia A gives off
a whole lot of radio signals.

202
00:16:19,146 --> 00:16:23,650
I actually listen to them a lot.
It's a remnant of a supernova.

203
00:16:24,359 --> 00:16:27,237
When did you know
you wanted to be an astronomer?

204
00:16:27,737 --> 00:16:29,197
Well, when I...

205
00:16:29,364 --> 00:16:33,201
When I was about 8 years old,
I was watching the sunset...

206
00:16:33,368 --> 00:16:36,746
...and I asked my dad,
"What's that bright star over there?"

207
00:16:36,913 --> 00:16:39,583
Dad, what's that star there?

208
00:16:40,167 --> 00:16:43,253
<i>And he said that it wasn't
really a star at all. It was actually...</i>

209
00:16:43,420 --> 00:16:45,088
That's not a star, it's a planet.

210
00:16:45,255 --> 00:16:46,882
<i>...a whole planet called Venus...</i>

211
00:16:47,048 --> 00:16:49,050
That's the planet Venus.

212
00:16:51,386 --> 00:16:55,348
...which should be over there soon.

213
00:16:55,515 --> 00:16:58,226
He said,
"You know why they called it Venus?

214
00:16:58,393 --> 00:17:01,688
Because they thought
it was so beautiful and glowing.

215
00:17:01,855 --> 00:17:03,940
But what they didn't know...

216
00:17:04,107 --> 00:17:06,735
...is that it's filled with...

217
00:17:07,944 --> 00:17:11,656
...deadly gases and sulfuric acid rain."

218
00:17:12,699 --> 00:17:16,912
"Wow," I thought,
"This is it! I'm hooked."

219
00:17:18,455 --> 00:17:22,459
You know, there are four hundred billion
stars out there...

220
00:17:22,626 --> 00:17:24,878
...just in our galaxy alone.

221
00:17:25,212 --> 00:17:29,132
If only one out of a million
of those had planets...

222
00:17:29,299 --> 00:17:32,469
...all right, and if just one
out of a million of those had life...

223
00:17:32,636 --> 00:17:37,265
...and if just one out of a million
of those had intelligent life...

224
00:17:37,557 --> 00:17:42,562
...there would be literally millions
of civilizations out there.

225
00:17:43,980 --> 00:17:46,066
Well, if there wasn't...

226
00:17:46,483 --> 00:17:48,985
...it'd be an awful waste of space.

227
00:17:54,574 --> 00:17:56,326
Amen.

228
00:18:04,668 --> 00:18:09,673
PALMER: So I was lying there,
just looking at the sky.

229
00:18:12,592 --> 00:18:16,429
And then I felt something. I don't know, I...

230
00:18:16,930 --> 00:18:19,808
All I know is that I wasn't alone.

231
00:18:19,975 --> 00:18:24,896
And for the first time in my life
I wasn't scared of nothing, you know?

232
00:18:25,063 --> 00:18:27,023
Not even dying.

233
00:18:32,362 --> 00:18:34,406
It was God.

234
00:18:36,866 --> 00:18:39,202
And there's no chance that...

235
00:18:39,369 --> 00:18:43,623
...you had this experience because
some part of you needed to have it?

236
00:18:44,874 --> 00:18:48,378
I mean, I'm a reasonably intelligent guy,
but this...

237
00:18:51,214 --> 00:18:55,218
No, this... No, my intellect,
it couldn't even touch this.

238
00:18:55,385 --> 00:18:56,428
No.

239
00:18:58,888 --> 00:19:01,474
I've been to Sunday school a few times.

240
00:19:02,892 --> 00:19:04,644
Uh-huh. And?

241
00:19:04,811 --> 00:19:10,066
Well, I just kept asking all these
really annoying questions like:

242
00:19:10,233 --> 00:19:13,194
"All right, then where did Mrs. Cain
come from?"

243
00:19:14,487 --> 00:19:17,073
And pretty soon they called my dad
and they asked him...

244
00:19:17,240 --> 00:19:19,993
...if he wouldn't mind just
keeping me home from now on.

245
00:19:20,160 --> 00:19:22,203
Your dad...

246
00:19:23,163 --> 00:19:26,374
- Is it this guy?
- Yeah.

247
00:19:28,251 --> 00:19:30,712
You're close to him, aren't you?

248
00:19:31,588 --> 00:19:35,050
Yeah, I was.
He died when I was 9 years old.

249
00:19:36,926 --> 00:19:38,928
I never got to know my mother.

250
00:19:40,513 --> 00:19:41,848
I'm sorry.

251
00:19:46,353 --> 00:19:48,271
That's gotta be tough.

252
00:19:50,482 --> 00:19:52,150
Yeah.

253
00:19:52,776 --> 00:19:54,778
Being alone.

254
00:20:03,286 --> 00:20:04,537
What do you say...

255
00:20:04,746 --> 00:20:08,249
...we pack a picnic tomorrow morning
and hike up to old Mount Arecibo?

256
00:20:09,125 --> 00:20:12,128
I don't think I can. I gotta work.

257
00:20:13,922 --> 00:20:17,550
All right, how about dinner, then,
tomorrow night? I know a great dive.

258
00:20:17,717 --> 00:20:19,677
I don't think so.

259
00:20:23,973 --> 00:20:27,143
- Ellie, did I miss something?
- Huh?

260
00:20:28,269 --> 00:20:31,481
No. Shit, I'm late.

261
00:20:32,440 --> 00:20:36,027
I told Kent that I'd be there at 10:30.

262
00:20:37,987 --> 00:20:39,781
You know, I'm not trying to push you.

263
00:20:39,989 --> 00:20:42,409
No, no. I've wanted to look at
this sector for weeks.

264
00:20:42,575 --> 00:20:47,831
Just hang out and sleep in.
And there's a bunch of food in the fridge.

265
00:20:47,997 --> 00:20:49,165
Okay?

266
00:20:49,332 --> 00:20:51,167
How can I reach you?

267
00:20:51,835 --> 00:20:54,337
Just leave your number. I'll call you.

268
00:21:15,817 --> 00:21:16,860
YOUNG ELLIE:
<i>Dad!</i>

269
00:21:18,903 --> 00:21:21,906
Dad, it's starting, you're gonna miss it.

270
00:21:25,034 --> 00:21:27,328
Dad, there's another one. Hurry up.

271
00:21:27,495 --> 00:21:29,873
TED:
In a minute, Sparks. I'm almost done.

272
00:21:30,039 --> 00:21:32,876
There's another one.
Come on, hurry up, Dad!

273
00:21:34,919 --> 00:21:36,921
[CLATTERING]

274
00:21:38,214 --> 00:21:39,883
Dad?

275
00:21:51,644 --> 00:21:52,812
Dad?

276
00:22:02,947 --> 00:22:04,032
Daddy?

277
00:22:17,003 --> 00:22:18,546
Dad.

278
00:22:21,758 --> 00:22:23,384
Dad.

279
00:22:24,719 --> 00:22:26,429
Dad.

280
00:22:28,431 --> 00:22:30,350
I'll get the medicine.

281
00:23:45,925 --> 00:23:47,594
Ellie.

282
00:23:49,178 --> 00:23:51,848
I know it's hard to understand this now...

283
00:23:52,849 --> 00:23:57,353
...but we aren't always meant to know
why things happen the way they do.

284
00:23:57,895 --> 00:24:02,233
Sometimes we just have to accept it
as God's will.

285
00:24:04,193 --> 00:24:07,864
I should have kept some medicine
in the downstairs bathroom.

286
00:24:08,031 --> 00:24:10,575
Then I could've gotten to it sooner.

287
00:24:26,549 --> 00:24:29,010
[CHATTERING]

288
00:24:36,100 --> 00:24:37,769
YOUNG ELLIE:
<i>CQ...</i>

289
00:24:38,603 --> 00:24:41,898
<i>...this is W-9 GFO. Do you copy?</i>

290
00:24:45,068 --> 00:24:47,737
Dad, this is Ellie. Come back.

291
00:24:50,907 --> 00:24:52,950
This is Eleanor Arroway...

292
00:24:53,117 --> 00:24:56,704
...transmitting on 14.2 megahertz.

293
00:24:59,207 --> 00:25:00,792
Dad.

294
00:25:02,835 --> 00:25:04,337
Are you there?

295
00:25:06,464 --> 00:25:07,924
Come back.

296
00:25:10,843 --> 00:25:12,261
Dad.

297
00:25:14,639 --> 00:25:16,099
Are you there?

298
00:25:17,141 --> 00:25:18,643
Dad, this is Ellie.

299
00:25:27,652 --> 00:25:30,154
[BEEPING]

300
00:25:48,005 --> 00:25:49,966
[DOOR OPENS AND CLOSES]

301
00:25:50,133 --> 00:25:51,426
Ellie.

302
00:25:51,592 --> 00:25:54,971
Hi, Kent. What are you doing
up so early, huh?

303
00:25:55,638 --> 00:25:56,973
I was thinking...

304
00:25:57,682 --> 00:25:59,225
...we'd make a pretty good team.

305
00:25:59,392 --> 00:26:00,435
What?

306
00:26:00,601 --> 00:26:04,105
We could put together
a kick-ass ad-hoc SETI program.

307
00:26:04,313 --> 00:26:06,524
You know the Very Large Array
in New Mexico?

308
00:26:06,691 --> 00:26:09,152
It would be a dynamite place
to do serious SETI...

309
00:26:09,318 --> 00:26:11,988
What are you talking about, Kent?

310
00:26:13,448 --> 00:26:15,324
Drumlin pulled the plug.

311
00:26:15,950 --> 00:26:17,076
We're homeless.

312
00:26:27,670 --> 00:26:29,464
Hey, watch it.

313
00:26:31,215 --> 00:26:33,134
ELLIE:
Is it true, huh? Did you pull the plug?

314
00:26:33,301 --> 00:26:35,678
You can't see it now,
but I'm doing you a favor.

315
00:26:35,845 --> 00:26:40,016
You are far too promising a scientist
to be wasting your gifts on this nonsense.

316
00:26:40,183 --> 00:26:42,185
Look, I don't consider what could be...

317
00:26:42,393 --> 00:26:45,396
...the most important discovery
of the human race nonsense, okay?

318
00:26:45,563 --> 00:26:46,981
There's 400 billion stars...

319
00:26:47,148 --> 00:26:50,026
And two probabilities.
One, there is intelligent life...

320
00:26:50,234 --> 00:26:53,237
...but it's so far away you'll
never contact it in your lifetime.

321
00:26:53,404 --> 00:26:55,615
- And two!
- You're making a decision...

322
00:26:55,782 --> 00:27:00,203
There's nothing but noble gases and carbon
compounds and you're wasting your time.

323
00:27:00,369 --> 00:27:03,456
In the meantime, you won't be published,
won't be taken seriously.

324
00:27:03,623 --> 00:27:05,333
And your career will be over.

325
00:27:05,500 --> 00:27:08,878
So what? It's my life.

326
00:27:45,915 --> 00:27:48,793
Hey, Kent. You were right.

327
00:27:48,960 --> 00:27:52,672
Screw Drumlin. We're gonna raise
the money. We're going to New Mexico.

328
00:27:52,839 --> 00:27:55,383
KENT: Chicago has all the stuff,
but let me know how it goes.

329
00:27:55,550 --> 00:27:58,427
I've got some more corporate stops
on the East Coast for you.

330
00:27:58,594 --> 00:28:01,764
Hey, Fish, did that investor from Houston
ever get back to us?

331
00:28:01,931 --> 00:28:03,933
FISHER:
I'm gonna hit him up on my way to L.A.

332
00:28:04,141 --> 00:28:06,269
ELLIE: Try begging for some of that
Hollywood money.

333
00:28:06,477 --> 00:28:10,064
Why not? They've been making money
off of aliens for years, right?

334
00:28:10,231 --> 00:28:13,067
- Begging is good for the soul.
- We'll see about that.

335
00:28:13,234 --> 00:28:14,902
- Just do me a favor.
- Yeah.

336
00:28:15,069 --> 00:28:17,864
Try not to be too confrontational.

337
00:28:18,030 --> 00:28:20,491
Huh? Confrontational? Me?
What do you mean?

338
00:28:20,658 --> 00:28:22,285
I'm gonna see if I left anything.

339
00:28:55,651 --> 00:28:59,488
ELLIE: <i>This is a unique time in our history,
in the history of any civilization.</i>

340
00:28:59,655 --> 00:29:02,450
<i>It's the moment
of the acquisition of technology.</i>

341
00:29:02,658 --> 00:29:05,494
<i>That's the moment
where contact becomes possible.</i>

342
00:29:06,203 --> 00:29:11,042
The Very Large Array in New Mexico
is the key to our chances for success.

343
00:29:11,208 --> 00:29:13,461
With its 27 linked radio telescopes...

344
00:29:13,628 --> 00:29:17,006
...we can search more accurately
than at any other conventional facility.

345
00:29:17,173 --> 00:29:22,345
Now, we've gotten approval to buy
the telescope time from the government.

346
00:29:22,845 --> 00:29:25,014
Now all we really need is the money.

347
00:29:25,806 --> 00:29:27,808
A nice presentation, doctor.

348
00:29:28,517 --> 00:29:33,022
But while our foundation arm has a mandate
to support experimental programs...

349
00:29:33,189 --> 00:29:37,151
...we must confess that
your proposal seems less like science...

350
00:29:37,318 --> 00:29:39,028
...and more like science fiction.

351
00:29:41,530 --> 00:29:42,990
Science fiction.

352
00:29:46,410 --> 00:29:48,204
You're right.

353
00:29:48,663 --> 00:29:50,247
It's crazy.

354
00:29:51,040 --> 00:29:54,001
In fact, it's even worse than that: nuts.

355
00:29:55,544 --> 00:29:57,046
You wanna hear something nutty?

356
00:29:57,213 --> 00:29:59,882
A couple guys wanted to build
something called an airplane.

357
00:30:00,049 --> 00:30:03,219
You get people to go in it and fly like birds.
Ridiculous, right?

358
00:30:03,386 --> 00:30:05,346
Or what about
breaking the sound barrier...

359
00:30:05,513 --> 00:30:09,141
...or rockets to the moon,
or atomic energy or a mission to Mars?

360
00:30:09,350 --> 00:30:11,185
Science fiction, right?

361
00:30:11,352 --> 00:30:12,478
Look.

362
00:30:12,645 --> 00:30:16,899
All I'm asking is for you to
just have the tiniest bit of vision.

363
00:30:17,066 --> 00:30:20,653
You know, to just step back for one minute
and look at the big picture.

364
00:30:20,820 --> 00:30:22,279
To take a chance on something...

365
00:30:22,446 --> 00:30:26,075
...which might end up being
the most profoundly impactful moment...

366
00:30:26,242 --> 00:30:30,413
...for humanity, for the history of history.

367
00:30:33,624 --> 00:30:35,167
I'm sorry.

368
00:30:36,293 --> 00:30:37,920
I just spent the last 13 months...

369
00:30:38,587 --> 00:30:41,924
...coming into rooms like this
and talking to people like you.

370
00:30:42,341 --> 00:30:45,052
The truth is, you're my last chance, so...

371
00:30:45,219 --> 00:30:46,679
I'm sorry I wasted your time.

372
00:30:46,846 --> 00:30:48,139
Doctor.

373
00:30:48,305 --> 00:30:49,473
Yes, sir.

374
00:30:52,435 --> 00:30:54,478
Yes, sir.

375
00:30:57,732 --> 00:30:58,774
Yes, sir.

376
00:31:01,861 --> 00:31:03,070
You have your money.

377
00:31:06,115 --> 00:31:07,658
Thank you.

378
00:31:09,285 --> 00:31:10,911
Thank you.

379
00:31:39,273 --> 00:31:41,776
[MOTOR HUMMING]

380
00:31:57,458 --> 00:32:01,504
MAN:
Oh! Perfect cast. Come on, fishy.

381
00:32:01,670 --> 00:32:05,341
Frank, look at the size
of that son of a bitch!

382
00:32:17,770 --> 00:32:19,855
Welcome back, Dr. C.

383
00:32:20,022 --> 00:32:21,524
How'd it go?

384
00:32:22,191 --> 00:32:25,444
- How was the trip?
- I've had better.

385
00:32:25,945 --> 00:32:29,031
Drumlin. What is this?
This his personal vendetta now?

386
00:32:29,198 --> 00:32:33,119
It's not just Drumlin. There's been enormous
pressure from other scientists as well.

387
00:32:33,285 --> 00:32:36,539
It doesn't matter anyway,
Hadden's funding us for another two years.

388
00:32:36,705 --> 00:32:39,875
Uh... These are government-owned
telescopes, they can lease to whomever.

389
00:32:40,042 --> 00:32:43,838
And they don't want the high priestess
of the desert using them anymore.

390
00:32:44,672 --> 00:32:45,714
What?

391
00:32:45,881 --> 00:32:49,218
Staring at static on TV for hours,
listening to washing machines.

392
00:32:49,385 --> 00:32:51,762
Did you really think these stories
wouldn't get out?

393
00:32:51,929 --> 00:32:55,683
- I was looking for patterns in the chaos.
- Doesn't matter, we're a joke to them.

394
00:32:55,850 --> 00:32:57,393
They want us out.

395
00:32:58,853 --> 00:33:02,189
We've got three months
until the paperwork goes through.

396
00:33:04,900 --> 00:33:08,070
Fine. I'll start looking for other funding.
I'll just start...

397
00:33:08,237 --> 00:33:12,741
Could you face reality, please?
Just this once, Ellie. We lost.

398
00:33:12,908 --> 00:33:14,577
It's over.

399
00:33:20,916 --> 00:33:22,585
Fine.

400
00:33:22,751 --> 00:33:24,753
But I'm not stopping.

401
00:33:24,920 --> 00:33:27,923
If I have to go it alone, I'll go it alone.
I've done it before.

402
00:34:17,681 --> 00:34:21,435
<i>My guest tonight is author
and theologian Palmer Joss.</i>

403
00:34:21,602 --> 00:34:26,607
<i>He's become a spiritual counselor of sorts
and a recent fixture at the White House.</i>

404
00:34:26,774 --> 00:34:29,735
<i>"God's Diplomat,"
according to</i> The New York Times.

405
00:34:29,944 --> 00:34:33,614
<i>His latest book,</i> Losing Faith,
<i>is currently number one...</i>

406
00:34:33,822 --> 00:34:36,158
<i>...on that publication's bestseller list.</i>

407
00:34:36,325 --> 00:34:37,826
<i>Thanks for being with us, Palmer.</i>

408
00:34:37,993 --> 00:34:40,788
<i>You have had quite a ride
this last couple of years.</i>

409
00:34:40,955 --> 00:34:43,415
<i>Yeah, I sure have, Larry.</i>

410
00:34:43,582 --> 00:34:46,085
You know, Fish, I was thinking.

411
00:34:46,252 --> 00:34:50,589
Who would make the best astronomers?
I mean, think about it.

412
00:34:50,756 --> 00:34:56,762
Who has the perfect synthesis
of career and lifestyle?

413
00:34:56,929 --> 00:34:59,848
- I give.
- Vampires!

414
00:35:00,015 --> 00:35:02,434
<i>Are you anti-technology?
Are you anti-science?</i>

415
00:35:02,601 --> 00:35:04,603
PALMER:
<i>No, not at all.</i>

416
00:35:04,770 --> 00:35:07,648
<i>The question that I'm asking is this:</i>

417
00:35:08,315 --> 00:35:11,026
<i>Are we happier as a human race?</i>

418
00:35:11,193 --> 00:35:16,115
<i>Is the world fundamentally a better place
because of science and technology?</i>

419
00:35:16,824 --> 00:35:19,285
<i>We shop at home, we surf the Web...</i>

420
00:35:19,493 --> 00:35:22,288
<i>...but at the same time we feel emptier...</i>

421
00:35:22,454 --> 00:35:27,293
<i>...and more cut off from each other
than at any other time in human history.</i>

422
00:35:27,459 --> 00:35:30,129
<i>I mean, we're becoming
a synthesized society...</i>

423
00:35:30,296 --> 00:35:33,632
<i>...in a great, big hurry
to get to the next...</i>

424
00:35:47,021 --> 00:35:50,524
<i>I think it's because we're looking
for the meaning. What is the meaning?</i>

425
00:35:50,691 --> 00:35:53,402
<i>We have mindless jobs,
we take frantic vacations.</i>

426
00:35:53,819 --> 00:35:56,822
<i>Deficit finance trips to the mall
to buy more things...</i>

427
00:35:57,031 --> 00:36:00,701
<i>...that we think are gonna fill
these holes in our lives.</i>

428
00:36:00,868 --> 00:36:03,412
<i>Is it any wonder that we've lost
our sense of direction?</i>

429
00:36:19,136 --> 00:36:23,891
[BUZZING AND PULSING]

430
00:36:44,286 --> 00:36:45,954
Holy shit.

431
00:36:59,301 --> 00:37:01,303
[ENGINE STARTS]

432
00:37:04,264 --> 00:37:10,312
Right ascension,
18 hours, 36 minutes, 56.2 seconds.

433
00:37:10,479 --> 00:37:15,484
Declination, plus 36 degrees,
46 minutes...

434
00:37:15,651 --> 00:37:18,821
<i>...56.2 seconds!</i>

435
00:37:20,489 --> 00:37:21,990
<i>Confirm!</i>

436
00:37:22,157 --> 00:37:28,080
Right ascension, 18 hours, 36 minutes,
56.2 seconds!

437
00:37:28,247 --> 00:37:31,083
<i>Hey, is anybody awake in there?
I'm moving the array!</i>

438
00:37:32,167 --> 00:37:34,962
<i>- Confirm!</i>
- Boss has a bogey!

439
00:37:35,170 --> 00:37:40,300
<i>Right ascension, 18 hours,
36 minutes, 56.2 seconds.</i>

440
00:37:40,467 --> 00:37:42,719
- Fish!
- We're on it.

441
00:37:42,886 --> 00:37:47,224
<i>Plus 36 hours,
47 minutes and 1 second.</i>

442
00:37:47,391 --> 00:37:51,145
Strong possible pulse chain
on Sub-Array 4.

443
00:37:51,353 --> 00:37:54,690
I'm moving the rest of them now.
I need you to confirm the coordinates.

444
00:37:54,857 --> 00:37:56,859
Check the status of the array.

445
00:37:57,025 --> 00:38:00,904
- She's doing all the dishes.
- All systems nominal.

446
00:38:05,534 --> 00:38:07,119
Hello.

447
00:38:07,870 --> 00:38:09,621
I need a full systems check.

448
00:38:09,788 --> 00:38:12,624
<i>Coordinates confirmed.
Willie's checking the system now.</i>

449
00:38:12,791 --> 00:38:15,794
Stay on top of the systems diagnostics.

450
00:38:15,961 --> 00:38:17,671
Check the reference point offsets.

451
00:38:17,880 --> 00:38:21,884
And I want you off-axis on 27
the second we get there.

452
00:38:22,050 --> 00:38:24,553
And tell Willie to break out the big boy.

453
00:38:26,138 --> 00:38:28,474
Copy that. Willie...

454
00:38:28,640 --> 00:38:29,892
Yeah. Go, Ellie.

455
00:38:30,058 --> 00:38:33,353
I can hear can it on the headphones.
We gotta be sure about this.

456
00:38:33,520 --> 00:38:36,231
Give me another array status.

457
00:38:37,649 --> 00:38:40,569
Her four are online,
the rest are tracking in.

458
00:38:40,736 --> 00:38:43,489
FISHER: Your four are online,
the rest are tracking in.

459
00:38:43,655 --> 00:38:46,033
But there's a bad drive on 16.

460
00:38:46,241 --> 00:38:48,744
Your four are online,
the rest are tracking in...

461
00:38:48,911 --> 00:38:51,747
<i>...but there's a bad drive on 16.
Copy that?</i>

462
00:38:52,998 --> 00:38:55,292
<i>What do you want me to do
about the source queue?</i>

463
00:38:55,459 --> 00:38:57,544
Look, forget about it and hold the queue.

464
00:38:57,711 --> 00:39:01,882
Go get ready to set the target frequency
to manual the second we're aligned.

465
00:39:02,090 --> 00:39:06,428
Do you copy? I want you to go
off-axis on 27 the second we're there.

466
00:39:06,678 --> 00:39:09,515
And leave the LO frequency where it is.

467
00:39:09,681 --> 00:39:12,976
Stay on it, Fish, don't let it get away.
If you lose it, scan the band.

468
00:39:13,143 --> 00:39:16,438
Run every frequency you can think of.

469
00:39:21,026 --> 00:39:22,736
How you doing?

470
00:39:26,949 --> 00:39:28,075
Talk to me, guys.

471
00:39:28,283 --> 00:39:32,120
Partially polarized set of moving pulses,
amplitude modulated.

472
00:39:32,287 --> 00:39:35,958
We're locked. Systems check out, signal
across the board. What's the frequency?

473
00:39:36,124 --> 00:39:39,169
4.46, 23 gigahertz.

474
00:39:40,546 --> 00:39:43,799
Hydrogen times pi. Told you.

475
00:39:43,966 --> 00:39:45,759
Strong sucker too.

476
00:39:45,926 --> 00:39:48,262
I got it! I got it, I got it!
I'm patched in.

477
00:39:48,971 --> 00:39:50,639
ELLIE:
All right, let me hear it.

478
00:39:53,767 --> 00:39:55,519
[PULSING]

479
00:39:55,686 --> 00:39:57,145
Listen to that.

480
00:39:59,398 --> 00:40:01,650
Make me a liar, Fish.

481
00:40:01,817 --> 00:40:04,486
It could be AWACS out of Kirtland
jamming us.

482
00:40:04,653 --> 00:40:06,029
But I'm doubting it.

483
00:40:06,196 --> 00:40:08,824
All right, let's see
if F.U.D.D.'s reading it too.

484
00:40:09,658 --> 00:40:12,995
Willie, patch it back
and give me the off-axis.

485
00:40:13,161 --> 00:40:14,830
- Are we recording?
- Never stopped.

486
00:40:16,915 --> 00:40:18,208
Thank you, Elmer.

487
00:40:18,417 --> 00:40:20,335
AWACS status is negative.

488
00:40:20,502 --> 00:40:23,338
- White Sands?
- On this frequency? No.

489
00:40:23,505 --> 00:40:25,007
I'm gonna punch up the darks.

490
00:40:28,176 --> 00:40:31,847
How's the spying tonight, guys?
Come on.

491
00:40:34,182 --> 00:40:36,685
FISHER: NORAD's not tracking
any snoops in this vector.

492
00:40:36,852 --> 00:40:38,437
Shuttle <i>Endeavor</i> is in sleep mode.

493
00:40:38,604 --> 00:40:41,106
Okay, point source confirmed.

494
00:40:41,273 --> 00:40:43,859
- Whatever it is, it ain't local.
- Position?

495
00:40:44,026 --> 00:40:46,695
WILLIE: I checked interferometry.
Somewhere in Lyra, I think.

496
00:40:46,862 --> 00:40:48,363
-Vega?
FISHER: Can't be.

497
00:40:48,530 --> 00:40:50,782
It's only 26 light years away.

498
00:40:50,949 --> 00:40:53,910
-What's the peak intensity?
FISHER: Coming up.

499
00:40:54,077 --> 00:40:55,329
ELLIE:
Vega.

500
00:40:55,495 --> 00:40:59,458
Vega. I scanned it a bunch of times at
Arecibo. It was negative results, always.

501
00:40:59,666 --> 00:41:03,045
Got it. Reading over 100 janskys.

502
00:41:03,211 --> 00:41:05,589
Jesus. I could pick it up on my...

503
00:41:05,756 --> 00:41:07,966
[PULSING STOPS]

504
00:41:08,717 --> 00:41:09,718
No.

505
00:41:40,248 --> 00:41:42,876
[PULSING]

506
00:41:43,043 --> 00:41:44,086
Come on.

507
00:41:46,421 --> 00:41:48,507
All right, it's restarting.

508
00:41:48,965 --> 00:41:50,717
Wait a minute. Those are numbers.

509
00:41:50,926 --> 00:41:54,012
That was a three,
the one before it was a two.

510
00:41:54,179 --> 00:41:57,599
Base-10 numbers. Just start counting now,
see how far can we get.

511
00:41:57,766 --> 00:41:59,393
One.

512
00:42:04,940 --> 00:42:06,024
WILLIE: Seven.
ELLIE: Seven.

513
00:42:08,860 --> 00:42:10,445
Those are primes.

514
00:42:10,612 --> 00:42:13,198
Two, three, five, seven,
those are all prime numbers.

515
00:42:13,365 --> 00:42:15,325
There's no way
that's a natural phenomenon.

516
00:42:15,492 --> 00:42:16,618
- Holy shit.
- Okay, okay.

517
00:42:16,785 --> 00:42:20,122
Let's just calm down
and pull up the star file on Vega.

518
00:42:20,664 --> 00:42:23,625
FISHER: It doesn't make any sense.
The system is too young...

519
00:42:23,792 --> 00:42:25,794
...so it can't have a planetary system...

520
00:42:25,961 --> 00:42:29,381
...let alone life
or a technological civilization.

521
00:42:29,548 --> 00:42:32,134
Maybe they didn't grow up there.
Maybe they're visiting.

522
00:42:32,300 --> 00:42:35,637
Spacecraft? This system is
full of debris. It would get clobbered.

523
00:42:35,804 --> 00:42:39,975
Well, not if they used their laser blasters
and photon torpedoes.

524
00:42:40,142 --> 00:42:43,562
- That's not funny.
- How else are we gonna explain it?

525
00:42:43,729 --> 00:42:46,940
Willie's right. If we go public with this
and we're wrong, that's it.

526
00:42:47,107 --> 00:42:49,609
It's over. We're cooked.

527
00:42:50,610 --> 00:42:53,196
God, I wish Kent was here.

528
00:42:53,363 --> 00:42:57,451
Whatever the signal is, we better
do something soon. Vega's gonna set.

529
00:42:57,993 --> 00:43:04,583
<i>Position confirmed.
We've got 4.4623 gigahertz, confirmed.</i>

530
00:43:04,750 --> 00:43:07,669
<i>We've got 112janskys.</i>

531
00:43:07,836 --> 00:43:10,297
All right, do you have
a source location yet?

532
00:43:12,841 --> 00:43:15,761
<i>We put it right smack in the middle.</i>

533
00:43:15,927 --> 00:43:17,512
<i>Vega.</i>

534
00:43:19,347 --> 00:43:22,350
Okay, thanks, Ian. Keep tracking
and we'll get back to you.

535
00:43:22,517 --> 00:43:24,186
<i>Yeah, right-o.</i>

536
00:43:34,362 --> 00:43:35,864
FISHER:
Okay, 101.

537
00:43:36,031 --> 00:43:40,786
The pulse sequenced through every
prime number between two and 101.

538
00:43:42,370 --> 00:43:44,372
Who are we gonna call now?

539
00:43:47,209 --> 00:43:48,293
Everybody.

540
00:43:56,051 --> 00:43:59,471
MAN 1 [ON TV]: <i>The rumors are flying,
the implications extraordinary.</i>

541
00:43:59,638 --> 00:44:03,266
<i>This morning's detection of an unidentified
radio source from deep space...</i>

542
00:44:03,433 --> 00:44:06,061
<i>...can neither be confirmed nor denied.</i>

543
00:44:06,228 --> 00:44:08,021
<i>But the military presence arriving...</i>

544
00:44:08,188 --> 00:44:12,067
MAN 2: Sources close to the VLA report
that the planet has been contacted...

545
00:44:12,234 --> 00:44:14,194
...by some form of intelligent being...

546
00:44:14,361 --> 00:44:17,864
...living outside of our solar system.
Again, there is no confirmation...

547
00:44:18,031 --> 00:44:21,952
...that the official IAU communiqué
was sent from this government facility.

548
00:44:22,118 --> 00:44:24,830
KITZ:
I want all of these people out of here.

549
00:44:24,996 --> 00:44:26,790
Where's the girl?

550
00:44:30,961 --> 00:44:33,880
Twenty-two stations worldwide
now confirming the signal.

551
00:44:34,047 --> 00:44:35,841
Vega is currently below our horizon...

552
00:44:36,007 --> 00:44:38,468
...so what you're hearing
is the signal from-

553
00:44:38,635 --> 00:44:42,430
Get decryption people in here now.
Lunacharsky is visiting at Cal Tech.

554
00:44:42,597 --> 00:44:44,057
Explain this to me.

555
00:44:44,266 --> 00:44:48,144
If the source of the signal is so
sophisticated, why the remedial math?

556
00:44:48,311 --> 00:44:50,105
Exactly, why don't they speak English?

557
00:44:50,272 --> 00:44:53,483
Maybe because 70 percent of the planet
speaks other languages.

558
00:44:53,650 --> 00:44:56,444
Mathematics is
the only truly universal language.

559
00:44:57,153 --> 00:44:59,739
It's no coincidence
that they're using primes.

560
00:44:59,948 --> 00:45:02,158
- I don't get it.
- Prime numbers.

561
00:45:02,325 --> 00:45:06,121
That would be integers that are
only divisible by themselves and one.

562
00:45:08,999 --> 00:45:12,627
We think this may be a beacon,
some kind of an announcement...

563
00:45:12,794 --> 00:45:13,920
...to get our attention.

564
00:45:14,129 --> 00:45:17,799
If it's attention you want, you got that.
One thing: Vega.

565
00:45:18,008 --> 00:45:21,511
People have been looking at Vega for years
with no results.

566
00:45:21,678 --> 00:45:24,514
And now yesterday they start
broadcasting primes. Why?

567
00:45:24,681 --> 00:45:28,560
Well, it's hardly yesterday, because the
signal has been transmitting for 26 years.

568
00:45:28,727 --> 00:45:30,186
Dr. Arroway.

569
00:45:30,353 --> 00:45:34,566
- Excuse me, who are you?
- Michael Kitz, National Security Advisor.

570
00:45:34,733 --> 00:45:36,026
Let me first say that your...

571
00:45:36,192 --> 00:45:40,155
Actually, first, could you ask the gentlemen
with the firearms to wait outside?

572
00:45:40,322 --> 00:45:42,324
This is supposed to be a civilian facility.

573
00:45:48,788 --> 00:45:51,416
- Doctor, with all due respect to your...
- Excuse me.

574
00:45:51,791 --> 00:45:55,128
Hey, hey, hey.
Hi, I'm so glad you're back.

575
00:45:55,712 --> 00:45:58,924
Come on, I got something for you to hear.
Is this great?

576
00:45:59,090 --> 00:46:01,509
- Who are all these people?
- I have no idea.

577
00:46:01,718 --> 00:46:04,679
Sit down. And we could use some help
with the spectrum analysis.

578
00:46:04,846 --> 00:46:06,806
- Listen to that. I'll get on it.
- Headsets.

579
00:46:06,973 --> 00:46:09,059
So I'll just come right to the point,
shall I?

580
00:46:09,351 --> 00:46:13,355
Your having sent this all over the world may
constitute a breach of national security.

581
00:46:13,521 --> 00:46:15,190
This isn't a person-to-person call.

582
00:46:15,398 --> 00:46:19,903
You can't think that a civilization sending
this would intend it just for Americans.

583
00:46:20,070 --> 00:46:21,905
I'm saying you might have consulted us.

584
00:46:22,072 --> 00:46:25,867
- The contents could be extremely sensitive.
- You want to classify prime numbers?

585
00:46:26,034 --> 00:46:29,788
Mike, please. We're only in line
with Vega so many hours a day.

586
00:46:29,955 --> 00:46:34,417
The only way to monitor the signal
is to get the cooperation of other stations.

587
00:46:34,584 --> 00:46:38,964
If Dr. Arroway hadn't acted quickly,
we could have lost key elements.

588
00:46:39,130 --> 00:46:40,757
Okay, fine, they've got the primes.

589
00:46:40,924 --> 00:46:44,052
If you're right about there being a more
significant transmission...

590
00:46:44,219 --> 00:46:45,887
Which will need help
to receive and decode.

591
00:46:46,054 --> 00:46:47,889
Doctor, do you understand that my job
is to protect...?

592
00:46:48,056 --> 00:46:49,849
KENT: Shh!
- What?

593
00:46:50,016 --> 00:46:52,102
- Listen.
- I hear it.

594
00:46:52,268 --> 00:46:54,020
- Hear what?
- Harmonic.

595
00:46:54,187 --> 00:46:58,441
Bingo. I had Willie ask Rattan
to retune to 8.9247 gigahertz.

596
00:46:58,608 --> 00:47:00,110
There's a lot more here, folks.

597
00:47:00,276 --> 00:47:03,238
-Fish, let's get on the negative sideband.
FISHER: I'm already on it.

598
00:47:03,405 --> 00:47:04,990
KITZ: What is going on?
DRUMLIN: Another signal.

599
00:47:05,156 --> 00:47:06,491
Another signal, look at that.

600
00:47:06,658 --> 00:47:09,786
A second signal at double the frequency.
It looks like a...

601
00:47:09,953 --> 00:47:11,329
Somebody get a TV monitor.

602
00:47:11,871 --> 00:47:13,957
Patch the recording
into the image processor.

603
00:47:14,124 --> 00:47:17,419
- You want the new data?
- Yeah, the new frequency we just recorded.

604
00:47:17,627 --> 00:47:19,546
David, explain this to me, please.

605
00:47:19,713 --> 00:47:22,841
With the prime there's another signal.
Looks like a TV transmission.

606
00:47:23,008 --> 00:47:24,175
WILLIE:
We're hot.

607
00:47:24,342 --> 00:47:27,429
Do me a favor, go get the blinds.
There is a lot of glare.

608
00:47:27,595 --> 00:47:29,097
You're patched in.

609
00:47:29,264 --> 00:47:33,435
All right, it's definitely an image.
Let's try and stabilize it.

610
00:47:37,480 --> 00:47:38,982
What do you make of that, Fish?

611
00:47:39,149 --> 00:47:42,569
It's two different interlaced frames.
I'm framing one.

612
00:47:45,947 --> 00:47:48,700
ELLIE: It's just noise.
FISHER: Trying frame two.

613
00:47:49,451 --> 00:47:53,204
I've got an offset carrier here.
I think it's audio.

614
00:47:53,371 --> 00:47:54,622
Plug it in, plug it in.

615
00:47:58,960 --> 00:48:00,253
Center that segment.

616
00:48:00,420 --> 00:48:01,963
FISHER:
Centering.

617
00:48:05,842 --> 00:48:08,803
-Can you clean it up any more than that?
FISHER: I'm working on it.

618
00:48:21,816 --> 00:48:23,151
What the hell...?

619
00:48:23,985 --> 00:48:25,153
Try zooming out.

620
00:48:29,032 --> 00:48:30,158
All right, freeze.

621
00:48:30,325 --> 00:48:31,659
Reverse values.

622
00:48:33,328 --> 00:48:35,455
Try zooming out again.

623
00:48:39,042 --> 00:48:41,169
Rotate 90 degrees counterclock...

624
00:48:42,670 --> 00:48:44,631
DRUMLIN:
My God.

625
00:48:45,924 --> 00:48:49,010
[HITLER SPEAKING IN GERMAN
ON RADIO]

626
00:48:49,677 --> 00:48:52,639
-Okay.
KENT: What's going on?

627
00:48:52,806 --> 00:48:55,475
You're not gonna believe this.

628
00:49:08,905 --> 00:49:10,657
Does anybody speak German?

629
00:49:12,075 --> 00:49:14,494
"I declare the Games in Berlin...

630
00:49:15,578 --> 00:49:17,330
...at the celebration...

631
00:49:17,539 --> 00:49:21,918
...of the first Olympics of the new era
has opened."

632
00:49:32,720 --> 00:49:34,139
Get me the White House.

633
00:49:34,764 --> 00:49:37,475
Twenty million people died
defeating that son of a bitch...

634
00:49:37,642 --> 00:49:39,853
...and he's our first ambassador
to outer space?

635
00:49:40,019 --> 00:49:42,355
- Actually...
- The broadcast from the '36 Olympics...

636
00:49:42,522 --> 00:49:45,525
...was the first television transmission
that went into space.

637
00:49:45,692 --> 00:49:49,070
That they recorded it and sent it back
is saying, "Hello, we heard you."

638
00:49:49,237 --> 00:49:51,698
Or, <i>"Sieg Heil,</i> you're our kind of people."

639
00:49:51,906 --> 00:49:54,576
Wait. Hitler and his politics
have nothing to do with this.

640
00:49:54,742 --> 00:49:58,246
It's highly unlikely they would understand,
and people need to understand...

641
00:49:58,413 --> 00:50:00,582
The people are in very good hands,
Dr. Arroway.

642
00:50:00,748 --> 00:50:01,749
We'll take it from here.

643
00:50:01,916 --> 00:50:04,002
WOMAN [ON TV]:
<i>The White House has just released...</i>

644
00:50:04,169 --> 00:50:05,253
Smile, folks. We're on.

645
00:50:05,420 --> 00:50:08,339
<i>...a statement confirming a message
from deep space...</i>

646
00:50:08,506 --> 00:50:12,594
<i>...has been received by American scientists.
Claire Shipman is at the White House.</i>

647
00:50:12,760 --> 00:50:15,930
<i>Donna, we've been told the president
will have only a few remarks...</i>

648
00:50:16,139 --> 00:50:17,599
<i>...about today's announcement...</i>

649
00:50:17,765 --> 00:50:21,186
<i>...and that he won't be taking
any questions from the press.</i>

650
00:50:26,149 --> 00:50:27,483
Good afternoon.

651
00:50:30,361 --> 00:50:34,574
I'm glad to be joined by my
science and technology advisor.

652
00:50:36,409 --> 00:50:39,245
This is the product
of years of exploration...

653
00:50:39,454 --> 00:50:42,665
...by some of the world's
most distinguished scientists.

654
00:50:42,874 --> 00:50:47,086
Like all discoveries, this one
will and should continue to be reviewed...

655
00:50:47,295 --> 00:50:50,048
...examined and scrutinized.

656
00:50:50,256 --> 00:50:53,343
It must be confirmed by other scientists.

657
00:50:53,509 --> 00:50:59,307
The fact that something of this magnitude
is being explored is another...

658
00:50:59,474 --> 00:51:03,353
Fish, what is the story over here?
I thought Ellie was supposed to be on.

659
00:51:03,561 --> 00:51:05,063
I don't know.

660
00:51:05,688 --> 00:51:08,650
Guys? Guys? Um...

661
00:51:09,442 --> 00:51:12,278
You know those interlaced frames
that we thought were noise?

662
00:51:13,112 --> 00:51:15,782
This has structure. I'm hearing structure.

663
00:51:17,158 --> 00:51:19,410
<i>If this discovery is confirmed...</i>

664
00:51:19,619 --> 00:51:23,248
<i>...it will be one of the most stunning
insights into our universe...</i>

665
00:51:23,456 --> 00:51:25,500
<i>...that science has ever uncovered.</i>

666
00:51:26,000 --> 00:51:31,172
Its implications are as far-reaching
and awe-inspiring as can be imagined.

667
00:51:31,381 --> 00:51:34,842
Even as it promises answers
to some of our oldest questions...

668
00:51:35,051 --> 00:51:38,471
...it poses still others,
even more fundamental.

669
00:51:38,680 --> 00:51:42,308
We will continue to listen closely
to what it has to say...

670
00:51:42,517 --> 00:51:46,062
...as we continue the search for answers
and for knowledge...

671
00:51:46,271 --> 00:51:51,943
...that is as old as humanity itself
but essential to our people's future.

672
00:51:52,277 --> 00:51:53,736
-Thank you.
MAN: Sir...

673
00:51:53,903 --> 00:51:56,030
Is there a considerable
global security threat?

674
00:51:56,197 --> 00:51:59,325
I'll answer your questions at the end,
but first a few corrections.

675
00:51:59,492 --> 00:52:03,496
The message was received Friday morning,
6:31 a.m. Mountain Standard Time.

676
00:52:03,705 --> 00:52:08,293
It was largely mathematical, and in spite of
reports you may have seen to the contrary...

677
00:52:08,459 --> 00:52:11,004
...appears to be completely benign
in nature.

678
00:52:11,212 --> 00:52:14,966
Let me say that again.
The message is completely benign.

679
00:52:15,133 --> 00:52:18,636
The president has been in touch
with heads of state from around the world...

680
00:52:18,803 --> 00:52:21,556
...and every possible security measure
is being taken.

681
00:52:21,723 --> 00:52:24,767
To better explain to you
the events of the last 48 hours...

682
00:52:24,976 --> 00:52:29,397
...I'm turning you over to the leader of the
team that made this remarkable discovery...

683
00:52:29,564 --> 00:52:33,735
...Dr. David Drumlin,
special science advisor to the president.

684
00:52:41,659 --> 00:52:44,495
Thank you, Rachel. Hello.

685
00:52:46,414 --> 00:52:48,875
In 1936
a very faint television signal...

686
00:52:49,083 --> 00:52:52,170
...transmitted the opening ceremonies
of the Olympic Games...

687
00:52:52,337 --> 00:52:56,007
...as a show
of German superior technology.

688
00:52:57,091 --> 00:52:59,177
That signal left Earth
at the speed of light...

689
00:52:59,344 --> 00:53:01,346
[PHONE RINGING]

690
00:53:01,512 --> 00:53:05,350
...and 26 years later arrived in the vicinity
of the star we call Vega.

691
00:53:06,267 --> 00:53:10,104
That signal has been sent back to us
hugely amplified...

692
00:53:10,271 --> 00:53:12,940
...an unmistakable sign of intelligence.

693
00:53:13,107 --> 00:53:14,609
ELLIE:
<i>You're online, Kent, go ahead.</i>

694
00:53:14,776 --> 00:53:18,988
KENT: <i>The Adolf transmission used 25 frames
per second but we're receiving 50.</i>

695
00:53:19,155 --> 00:53:23,117
<i>So we canned the other 25
through a series of search algorithms.</i>

696
00:53:23,284 --> 00:53:25,828
<i>We found a series of markers
on the top of each frame.</i>

697
00:53:25,995 --> 00:53:27,705
<i>- Are you ready?</i>
- Yeah, go ahead.

698
00:53:27,872 --> 00:53:31,125
<i>-Send it, Willie.</i>
WILLIE: <i>Okay, here it comes.</i>

699
00:53:34,379 --> 00:53:35,713
KENT:
<i>It's digital...</i>

700
00:53:35,880 --> 00:53:40,134
<i>...massive amounts of data which
extend right to the higher harmonics.</i>

701
00:53:40,802 --> 00:53:42,553
Jackpot.

702
00:53:43,471 --> 00:53:45,390
ELLIE:
All right, as you'll see...

703
00:53:45,556 --> 00:53:48,851
...interlaced with the frames
of the original Hitler image...

704
00:53:49,018 --> 00:53:51,979
...we found these sections.

705
00:53:52,146 --> 00:53:55,650
Now, we thought they were just noise,
but they're actually data...

706
00:53:55,817 --> 00:53:57,110
...huge amounts of it.

707
00:53:57,276 --> 00:54:01,823
When we combine this with the data from
the amplification of the original signal...

708
00:54:01,989 --> 00:54:05,284
...we get these encrypted pages of text.

709
00:54:05,493 --> 00:54:09,664
Now, no two are alike and
we've uncovered over 10,000 already.

710
00:54:09,831 --> 00:54:12,250
What does it mean, doctor?

711
00:54:12,417 --> 00:54:14,752
Well, we have no idea.
It could be anything.

712
00:54:14,919 --> 00:54:17,630
It could be the first volume
of an <i>Encyclopedia Galactica.</i>

713
00:54:17,797 --> 00:54:20,716
Instructions to acquaint us
with their colonization procedures.

714
00:54:20,883 --> 00:54:22,844
Moses with a few billion
new commandments.

715
00:54:23,010 --> 00:54:25,304
How long will it take to decode it?

716
00:54:25,513 --> 00:54:28,850
It could take forever.
We really need a primer...

717
00:54:29,016 --> 00:54:32,103
We need to get the best
decryption people we have.

718
00:54:32,603 --> 00:54:36,858
I intend to use the VLA
as my command post.

719
00:54:37,024 --> 00:54:40,445
In the meantime, my office
has made out a preliminary budget.

720
00:54:40,611 --> 00:54:44,574
Wait a minute.
This is a privately-funded operation.

721
00:54:44,782 --> 00:54:47,076
We're only leasing dish time
from the government...

722
00:54:47,285 --> 00:54:50,371
If there's a more clear case
of eminent domain, I've never seen it.

723
00:54:50,538 --> 00:54:53,124
I'm going to recommend
we militarize this project.

724
00:54:53,291 --> 00:54:55,668
What? This is my project.

725
00:54:55,835 --> 00:54:59,213
Nobody knows more about these scenarios
than I do. David, tell them...

726
00:54:59,380 --> 00:55:02,758
Excuse me, could we calm down here?

727
00:55:02,967 --> 00:55:07,638
Now, I'm going to recommend that
Dr. Drumlin coordinate the decryption effort.

728
00:55:07,805 --> 00:55:11,184
However, in light of Dr. Arroway's
long experience in these matters...

729
00:55:11,350 --> 00:55:15,563
...for the time being, she will continue
to direct operations at the VLA...

730
00:55:15,730 --> 00:55:20,401
...understanding that future discoveries
will be kept in strictest confidence...

731
00:55:20,568 --> 00:55:22,487
...until such time as the president...

732
00:55:22,653 --> 00:55:26,657
...can decide the most suitable
course of action. Understood?

733
00:55:26,824 --> 00:55:28,034
Yes.

734
00:55:28,326 --> 00:55:30,953
MAN:
<i>This is CNN Breaking News.</i>

735
00:55:31,120 --> 00:55:34,081
<i>The president made a statement,
calling the message from Vega...</i>

736
00:55:34,290 --> 00:55:36,417
<i>...one of the most stunning insights
into our...</i>

737
00:55:36,626 --> 00:55:41,631
<i>Attendance at religious services has
risen a dramatic 39 percent in recent days.</i>

738
00:55:41,797 --> 00:55:45,635
<i>...as police in three German divisions
clashed with neo-Nazi protesters.</i>

739
00:55:45,801 --> 00:55:47,929
<i>So it turns out
there's life on other planets.</i>

740
00:55:48,137 --> 00:55:51,766
<i>Boy, this is really gonna change
the Miss Universe contest.</i>

741
00:55:52,433 --> 00:55:56,771
<i>Health officials from around the world are
concerned that the message from Vega...</i>

742
00:55:56,938 --> 00:55:59,565
<i>...might trigger a rash of mass suicides...</i>

743
00:55:59,774 --> 00:56:02,401
<i>...not unlike the recent cult deaths
near San Diego...</i>

744
00:56:02,610 --> 00:56:04,570
<i>Even a scientist has to admit...</i>

745
00:56:04,779 --> 00:56:09,283
<i>...that there are some pretty serious
religious overtones to all this.</i>

746
00:56:09,450 --> 00:56:11,619
<i>Let's hear from someone
whose organization...</i>

747
00:56:11,827 --> 00:56:14,372
<i>...has been accused of conducting
its own inquisition:</i>

748
00:56:14,539 --> 00:56:17,166
<i>Richard Rank,
head of the Conservative coalition.</i>

749
00:56:17,333 --> 00:56:18,417
<i>Thank you.</i>

750
00:56:18,584 --> 00:56:22,964
<i>This is yet another example of science
intruding into matters of faith.</i>

751
00:56:24,006 --> 00:56:27,301
<i>This means they
have been reacting to TV signals...</i>

752
00:56:27,510 --> 00:56:30,429
<i>...they got from the 1930s.
They're just getting them.</i>

753
00:56:30,638 --> 00:56:34,308
<i>Imagine how disappointed
they're gonna be if they're Cubs fans.</i>

754
00:56:34,475 --> 00:56:37,645
<i>We now take you to Jeremy Roth,
who is reporting on events...</i>

755
00:56:37,853 --> 00:56:41,315
<i>...at the Very Large Array
near Socorro, New Mexico.</i>

756
00:56:41,524 --> 00:56:43,359
<i>Like a bolt from the blue it came.</i>

757
00:56:43,526 --> 00:56:47,697
<i>The message from Vega has caused
thousands of believers and non-believers...</i>

758
00:56:47,863 --> 00:56:51,784
<i>...to descend upon the VLA facility
here in the desert of New Mexico.</i>

759
00:56:51,993 --> 00:56:54,495
<i>Many have come to protest,
many to pray...</i>

760
00:56:54,662 --> 00:56:59,500
<i>...but most have come to participate
in what has become the best show in town.</i>

761
00:57:06,132 --> 00:57:09,260
[CHATTERING]

762
00:57:26,360 --> 00:57:28,279
[CHANTING]

763
00:57:59,518 --> 00:58:05,399
That's right. I'll be there in Vega,
thank you very much.

764
00:58:08,402 --> 00:58:11,322
CHORUS [SINGING]: <i>Hail to Vega
Hail to Vega, Hail to Vega</i>

765
00:58:11,489 --> 00:58:14,742
<i>Hail to Vega</i>

766
00:58:16,077 --> 00:58:17,745
But they have failed!

767
00:58:18,329 --> 00:58:20,873
It's the same people who,
again and again...

768
00:58:21,040 --> 00:58:23,459
...have brought us
to the brink of destruction.

769
00:58:23,668 --> 00:58:27,713
Who've polluted our air,
who've poisoned our water.

770
00:58:28,923 --> 00:58:30,925
Now these scientists...

771
00:58:31,926 --> 00:58:33,761
...have had their chance.

772
00:58:33,928 --> 00:58:37,348
Are these the kind of people...

773
00:58:37,515 --> 00:58:40,643
...that you want
talking to your God for you?

774
00:58:40,810 --> 00:58:42,770
[PEOPLE YELLING INDISTINCTLY]

775
00:58:45,106 --> 00:58:47,191
MAN [ON TV]:
<i>Efforts continue around the clock.</i>

776
00:58:47,525 --> 00:58:51,112
<i>But for now, almost 2 full weeks
after the message's arrival...</i>

777
00:58:51,278 --> 00:58:54,448
<i>...scientists here at the VLA
still have no answers...</i>

778
00:58:54,615 --> 00:58:56,367
<i>...only more questions.</i>

779
00:58:56,534 --> 00:59:00,246
<i>Jeremy Roth, CNN,
Soccorro, New Mexico.</i>

780
00:59:00,413 --> 00:59:03,749
<i>More on the quest
for the message's meaning, after this.</i>

781
00:59:06,585 --> 00:59:08,337
They gotta line up somehow.

782
00:59:08,504 --> 00:59:10,464
Yeah, well, this one doesn't fit, either.

783
00:59:10,631 --> 00:59:13,300
We've tried over a million permutations.

784
00:59:13,467 --> 00:59:16,971
Always, I can only get
three corners to fit.

785
00:59:17,972 --> 00:59:20,099
I don't understand their significance.

786
01:00:01,974 --> 01:00:04,685
What...? Somebody broke in...

787
01:00:06,687 --> 01:00:08,731
[PHONE RINGS]

788
01:00:11,025 --> 01:00:12,193
Hello?

789
01:00:13,110 --> 01:00:16,530
Hey, listen, I don't know who you are,
but you broke into the database...

790
01:00:19,366 --> 01:00:21,076
What? Now?

791
01:00:23,537 --> 01:00:24,705
Where?

792
01:00:24,872 --> 01:00:26,791
[FAX MACHINE HUMMING]

793
01:00:37,718 --> 01:00:39,136
I'll be there.

794
01:01:06,747 --> 01:01:08,082
MAN 1:
You should feel fortunate.

795
01:01:08,833 --> 01:01:10,334
He hardly lands for anyone.

796
01:01:41,907 --> 01:01:43,868
MAN 2:
Dr. Arroway...

797
01:01:47,788 --> 01:01:49,123
...I presume?

798
01:01:50,416 --> 01:01:51,542
Mr. Hadden.

799
01:01:52,835 --> 01:01:56,088
I've had my eye on you
a long time, doctor.

800
01:01:56,881 --> 01:02:00,384
I consider you one of my
most valuable long-term investments.

801
01:02:02,261 --> 01:02:04,638
And when it comes to my investments...

802
01:02:06,432 --> 01:02:09,101
...I always do my homework.

803
01:02:13,814 --> 01:02:15,566
Eleanor Ann Arroway...

804
01:02:15,774 --> 01:02:18,277
...born 25th August, 1964...

805
01:02:18,485 --> 01:02:20,070
...De Pere, Wisconsin.

806
01:02:20,279 --> 01:02:24,491
Mother, Joanna, died
from complications during childbirth.

807
01:02:25,200 --> 01:02:27,912
Early testing indicated
high predisposition...

808
01:02:28,078 --> 01:02:31,165
...towards science and mathematics.

809
01:02:31,832 --> 01:02:33,000
Father, Theodore...

810
01:02:33,167 --> 01:02:36,712
...advised to provide
enrichment activities along these lines...

811
01:02:36,921 --> 01:02:39,673
...did so conscientiously
until his death...

812
01:02:39,840 --> 01:02:44,511
...from myocardial infarction,
November 10, 1974.

813
01:02:45,095 --> 01:02:48,349
You graduated from
high school in 1979...

814
01:02:48,515 --> 01:02:50,643
...almost two full years early.

815
01:02:52,061 --> 01:02:56,440
Awarded full scholarship, M.I.T,
graduated magna cum laude.

816
01:02:57,399 --> 01:02:59,109
Doctoral work, Cal Tech...

817
01:02:59,318 --> 01:03:02,655
...where you did breakthrough work
on the lanthanide-doped ruby maser...

818
01:03:02,821 --> 01:03:06,951
...dramatically increasing
the sensitivity of radio telescopes.

819
01:03:07,284 --> 01:03:10,996
Subsequently offered a teaching position
at Harvard University...

820
01:03:11,205 --> 01:03:14,291
...which you turned down
to pursue SETI work...

821
01:03:14,500 --> 01:03:17,920
...at the Arecibo Observatory
in Puerto Rico.

822
01:03:20,339 --> 01:03:26,595
Changes in NSF policy
gave rise to certain funding problems...

823
01:03:27,680 --> 01:03:31,684
...at which point
you came to my attention.

824
01:03:33,018 --> 01:03:35,229
You compromised our security codes.

825
01:03:37,690 --> 01:03:39,525
Once upon a time...

826
01:03:41,527 --> 01:03:43,779
...I was a hell of an engineer.

827
01:03:43,946 --> 01:03:46,365
Please, sit, doctor.

828
01:03:46,532 --> 01:03:51,662
I have guests so rarely, it's important to me
that they feel welcome in my home.

829
01:03:52,246 --> 01:03:53,998
You live here?

830
01:03:55,207 --> 01:03:58,544
I find it convenient
to keep my interests...

831
01:03:59,712 --> 01:04:00,838
...mobile.

832
01:04:03,424 --> 01:04:08,345
Anyway, I've had my fill
of life on the ground.

833
01:04:08,846 --> 01:04:10,973
Well, I... I guess I should...

834
01:04:11,432 --> 01:04:15,227
Well, I guess I should thank you
for having bailed me out all that time.

835
01:04:20,566 --> 01:04:22,693
I know a good bet when I see one.

836
01:04:25,070 --> 01:04:28,282
Earl Grey. No sugar, no milk, I believe.

837
01:04:31,243 --> 01:04:33,078
What am I doing here?

838
01:04:34,079 --> 01:04:36,457
[CHUCKLES]

839
01:04:43,422 --> 01:04:46,550
The powers that be
have been very busy lately...

840
01:04:47,384 --> 01:04:52,723
...falling over each other to position
themselves for the game of the millennium.

841
01:04:58,979 --> 01:05:01,774
Maybe I can help deal you back in.

842
01:05:03,025 --> 01:05:05,444
I didn't realize that I was out.

843
01:05:05,944 --> 01:05:08,238
No, maybe not out...

844
01:05:08,947 --> 01:05:11,241
...but certainly being handed your hat.

845
01:05:16,663 --> 01:05:20,334
I've had a long time
to make enemies, doctor.

846
01:05:23,921 --> 01:05:27,758
And there are many governments,
business interests...

847
01:05:27,925 --> 01:05:31,929
...even religious leaders that
would like to see me depart this earth.

848
01:05:33,388 --> 01:05:35,933
And I'll grant them their wish
soon enough.

849
01:05:36,475 --> 01:05:40,020
But before I do,
I wish to make a small contribution.

850
01:05:40,771 --> 01:05:45,359
A final gesture of goodwill to the
people of this little planet that have given...

851
01:05:48,153 --> 01:05:50,948
From whom I have taken so much.

852
01:05:54,409 --> 01:05:55,869
You found the primer.

853
01:05:58,288 --> 01:06:00,165
Clever girl.

854
01:06:00,332 --> 01:06:01,375
Lights.

855
01:06:09,967 --> 01:06:13,137
Pages and pages of data.

856
01:06:13,303 --> 01:06:16,431
Over 63,000 in all.

857
01:06:16,598 --> 01:06:18,684
And on the perimeter of each...

858
01:06:18,851 --> 01:06:22,479
Alignment symbols, a registration mark,
but they don't line up.

859
01:06:23,063 --> 01:06:26,233
They do, if you think like a Vegan.

860
01:06:27,151 --> 01:06:30,154
An alien intelligence
has got be more advanced...

861
01:06:30,320 --> 01:06:34,408
...and that means efficiency
functioning on multiple levels...

862
01:06:34,575 --> 01:06:38,412
...and in multiple dimensions.

863
01:06:42,374 --> 01:06:44,793
Yes, of course!

864
01:06:44,960 --> 01:06:46,003
Where's the primer?

865
01:06:46,503 --> 01:06:47,921
You'll see.

866
01:06:48,422 --> 01:06:52,885
Every three-dimensional page
contains a piece of the primer.

867
01:06:55,053 --> 01:06:58,557
There it was all the time,
staring you in the face.

868
01:06:59,641 --> 01:07:03,562
Buried within the message itself
is the key to decoding it.

869
01:07:03,729 --> 01:07:06,982
Within the layering of the matrix,
we have these basic equations:

870
01:07:08,942 --> 01:07:12,988
So with this very elementary foundation,
they have given us a kind of...

871
01:07:13,155 --> 01:07:15,741
...general scientific vocabulary.

872
01:07:15,949 --> 01:07:18,243
We now have the symbols
for true and false...

873
01:07:18,452 --> 01:07:19,745
This was the key...

874
01:07:19,912 --> 01:07:22,414
...that allowed us
to decipher their language...

875
01:07:22,623 --> 01:07:26,293
...for physics, geometry, chemistry.
Next frame, Ellie.

876
01:07:27,502 --> 01:07:31,465
Yes, and when we apply this
to the rest of the message...

877
01:07:32,216 --> 01:07:33,300
...we find this.

878
01:07:37,888 --> 01:07:40,807
Those look like engineering schematics,
almost like blueprints.

879
01:07:40,974 --> 01:07:42,601
Yeah.

880
01:07:42,768 --> 01:07:43,852
Yes.

881
01:07:44,019 --> 01:07:47,314
It is our belief that
the message contains instructions...

882
01:07:47,522 --> 01:07:50,984
...for building something,
some kind of machine.

883
01:07:51,151 --> 01:07:54,279
A machine? That does what, doctor?

884
01:07:54,446 --> 01:07:56,657
We don't know.
It might be some type...

885
01:07:57,616 --> 01:08:03,163
...of advanced communication device,
or a teaching machine of some kind.

886
01:08:03,664 --> 01:08:06,959
Or it might turn out to be
some kind of a transport.

887
01:08:07,376 --> 01:08:09,836
- Transport?
- There's no proof of that.

888
01:08:10,003 --> 01:08:13,840
The fact is, you don't know what it does.
It could be anything.

889
01:08:14,007 --> 01:08:17,344
Could be a Trojan horse. We build it
and out pours the entire Vegan army.

890
01:08:17,511 --> 01:08:20,180
Why bother risking the personnel?
It could be a weapon.

891
01:08:20,389 --> 01:08:22,683
Right. Some kind of
a doomsday machine.

892
01:08:22,849 --> 01:08:25,352
Exactly. Every time they detect
a new civilization...

893
01:08:25,519 --> 01:08:27,980
...they fax these construction documents
from space.

894
01:08:28,146 --> 01:08:31,775
We poor saps build whatever this is
and blow ourselves to kingdom come.

895
01:08:31,942 --> 01:08:34,861
There's no reason to believe
that their intentions are hostile.

896
01:08:35,028 --> 01:08:39,366
Why is the default position of the egghead
set that aliens would always be benign?

897
01:08:39,533 --> 01:08:41,994
- Why is that, doctor?
- We pose no threat to them.

898
01:08:42,160 --> 01:08:46,581
It would be like us going out of our way
to destroy microbes on an anthill in Africa

899
01:08:46,748 --> 01:08:48,333
DRUMLIN:
That's an interesting analogy.

900
01:08:48,750 --> 01:08:52,921
And how guilty would we feel if we
destroyed a few microbes on an anthill?

901
01:08:53,088 --> 01:08:55,465
I hope you're right, doctor. I really do.

902
01:08:55,632 --> 01:08:59,094
But right now my job is to protect
American lives from any threat.

903
01:08:59,261 --> 01:09:01,596
In that regard, I am obligated
to assume the worst.

904
01:09:01,847 --> 01:09:03,932
Well, yes, and I am forced
to agree with you.

905
01:09:04,099 --> 01:09:05,517
My problem is this:

906
01:09:06,143 --> 01:09:09,354
The content of that message
is morally ambiguous at best.

907
01:09:10,022 --> 01:09:13,567
Now, our internal numbers show
support for this is incredibly soft.

908
01:09:13,734 --> 01:09:16,403
If the president wishes
to stay this course...

909
01:09:16,570 --> 01:09:19,239
...you're gonna find his numbers
going south like a duck.

910
01:09:19,406 --> 01:09:21,825
-This is nuts.
RANK: Excuse me...

911
01:09:21,992 --> 01:09:23,410
...miss.

912
01:09:23,577 --> 01:09:25,954
We know nothing
of these creatures' values.

913
01:09:26,121 --> 01:09:28,915
The fact is, we don't even know
whether they believe in God.

914
01:09:29,082 --> 01:09:32,836
-This doesn't make any sense. If you...
CONSTANTINE: Excuse me, Dr. Arroway.

915
01:09:33,045 --> 01:09:35,505
We won't be suppressing
any opinions here today.

916
01:09:36,381 --> 01:09:39,092
No, of course not. I understand.

917
01:09:39,509 --> 01:09:41,428
What I meant to say...

918
01:09:41,595 --> 01:09:45,057
...is the message was written
in the language of science.

919
01:09:45,223 --> 01:09:48,602
Now, if it had been religious in nature,
it should have taken the form...

920
01:09:48,769 --> 01:09:51,396
...of a burning bush
or a booming voice from the sky.

921
01:09:51,605 --> 01:09:53,690
But a voice from the sky...

922
01:09:54,775 --> 01:09:57,611
...is exactly what you found,
Doctor Arroway.

923
01:10:00,989 --> 01:10:02,949
I'm sorry I'm late, ma'am.

924
01:10:03,116 --> 01:10:08,330
Now, I agree with Mr. Rank
that there are unavoidable...

925
01:10:08,538 --> 01:10:10,707
...religious implications at stake here.

926
01:10:10,874 --> 01:10:13,293
But that doesn't justify
taking an alarmist position.

927
01:10:15,337 --> 01:10:18,090
My hope here is that we can find
a common ground...

928
01:10:18,256 --> 01:10:20,967
...break through any obstacles
that may have kept us apart.

929
01:10:21,134 --> 01:10:24,554
KITZ: Well, perhaps...
- Listen, I need to make one thing very clear.

930
01:10:24,763 --> 01:10:29,393
Our organization will not be placated
with flowery New Age rhetoric...

931
01:10:30,394 --> 01:10:31,895
Mr. President.

932
01:10:33,355 --> 01:10:35,649
<i>The President met
with his top advisors today...</i>

933
01:10:35,816 --> 01:10:39,236
<i>...regarding the latest efforts
to decode the message.</i>

934
01:10:39,403 --> 01:10:44,074
<i>While there has been some progress,
the official line remains a cautious one.</i>

935
01:10:44,241 --> 01:10:48,870
<i>If you're asking when we'll find out
what the purpose of that machine is...</i>

936
01:10:49,871 --> 01:10:52,582
<i>...maybe tomorrow,
maybe next year, maybe never.</i>

937
01:10:57,003 --> 01:11:01,591
There he is. The man with his finger
on the spiritual pulse of the nation.

938
01:11:02,968 --> 01:11:06,388
- It's so good to see you, Ellie.
- You too.

939
01:11:07,639 --> 01:11:11,601
Your discovery, all of this.
It must be a real vindication.

940
01:11:12,269 --> 01:11:13,937
I'm really happy for you.

941
01:11:14,563 --> 01:11:18,525
- Are you?
- Mr. Joss, the president is waiting.

942
01:11:19,901 --> 01:11:23,029
- Okay. Yeah.
- The president is waiting.

943
01:11:26,950 --> 01:11:30,829
- Ellie, see you at the reception tonight?
- Yeah, I wouldn't miss it.

944
01:11:30,996 --> 01:11:32,873
All right, great.

945
01:11:42,883 --> 01:11:45,218
Excuse me, Miss Constantine,
may I speak to you?

946
01:11:45,385 --> 01:11:46,803
Certainly.

947
01:11:48,555 --> 01:11:50,765
- I have a big problem.
- Yes?

948
01:11:50,932 --> 01:11:52,434
Um...

949
01:11:52,601 --> 01:11:55,395
Do you know where I can find,
like, a really great dress?

950
01:12:02,402 --> 01:12:04,905
[PEOPLE YELLING INDISTINCTLY]

951
01:12:12,078 --> 01:12:18,919
God created man in the form of himself,
not in the form of monkeys!

952
01:12:20,253 --> 01:12:22,422
MAN 1: The Lord is God!
MAN 2: Praise God.

953
01:12:22,589 --> 01:12:26,801
MAN 3: Praise the Lord!
MAN 4: You're wasting my tax dollars!

954
01:12:27,594 --> 01:12:31,223
Get a job.
Don't waste my tax dollars.

955
01:12:31,389 --> 01:12:33,141
You hear me?

956
01:12:52,118 --> 01:12:53,912
Wow.

957
01:12:56,456 --> 01:12:58,124
You look beautiful, Ellie.

958
01:12:59,793 --> 01:13:02,128
- So do you.
- You really do.

959
01:13:05,799 --> 01:13:07,259
I read your book.

960
01:13:08,218 --> 01:13:09,719
Here we go.

961
01:13:09,886 --> 01:13:11,972
Would you like me to quote you?

962
01:13:12,138 --> 01:13:17,143
"Ironically, the thing that people
are most hungry for, meaning...

963
01:13:17,310 --> 01:13:20,647
...is the one thing that science
hasn't been able to give them."

964
01:13:20,814 --> 01:13:23,692
Yeah. Yeah.

965
01:13:24,317 --> 01:13:29,114
Come on. It's like you're saying
that science killed God.

966
01:13:29,281 --> 01:13:32,492
What if science simply revealed...

967
01:13:32,659 --> 01:13:35,161
...that he never existed
in the first place?

968
01:13:38,498 --> 01:13:41,668
- I think we're gonna need to get some air.
- What?

969
01:13:41,835 --> 01:13:43,670
And a few more of these.

970
01:13:45,088 --> 01:13:47,340
- Thank you.
- You're welcome.

971
01:13:48,341 --> 01:13:51,511
- A little chilly out here.
- Yeah, this is nice.

972
01:13:51,678 --> 01:13:53,054
- I got one for you.
- What?

973
01:13:53,221 --> 01:13:54,973
Occam's razor. Ever heard of it?

974
01:13:55,181 --> 01:13:57,851
Hoccam's razor.
Sounds like some slasher movie.

975
01:13:58,018 --> 01:14:01,605
No, Occam's Razor.
It's a basic scientific principle. It says...

976
01:14:01,771 --> 01:14:05,358
...all things being equal, the simplest
explanation tends to be the right one.

977
01:14:05,525 --> 01:14:07,152
- Makes sense to me.
- All right.

978
01:14:07,319 --> 01:14:10,113
- So, what's more likely? Thank you.
- You're welcome.

979
01:14:10,280 --> 01:14:12,907
An all-powerful, mysterious God
created the universe...

980
01:14:13,116 --> 01:14:15,952
...and then decided not to give
any proof of his existence?

981
01:14:16,119 --> 01:14:20,957
Or that he simply doesn't exist at all
and that we created him...

982
01:14:21,124 --> 01:14:23,627
...so we wouldn't have to feel
so small and alone.

983
01:14:24,544 --> 01:14:25,795
I don't know.

984
01:14:25,962 --> 01:14:29,966
I couldn't imagine living in a world
where God didn't exist.

985
01:14:30,634 --> 01:14:32,302
I wouldn't want to.

986
01:14:32,969 --> 01:14:35,388
How do you know that
you're not deluding yourself?

987
01:14:36,056 --> 01:14:39,309
I mean, for me, I'd need proof.

988
01:14:40,143 --> 01:14:41,645
Proof?

989
01:14:44,522 --> 01:14:47,025
- Did you love your father?
- What?

990
01:14:47,192 --> 01:14:48,985
Your dad, did you love him?

991
01:14:49,986 --> 01:14:52,822
Yes. Very much.

992
01:14:55,659 --> 01:14:57,202
Prove it.

993
01:14:59,996 --> 01:15:01,915
[PHONE RINGS]

994
01:15:03,708 --> 01:15:04,751
Kent.

995
01:15:04,918 --> 01:15:05,919
[PAGER BEEPING]

996
01:15:06,086 --> 01:15:09,089
- The White House.
- Mr. Joss.

997
01:15:10,256 --> 01:15:13,218
DRUMLIN: I don't want excuses.
Whoever is responsible, I want their head.

998
01:15:13,385 --> 01:15:16,346
- Ellie?
- What happened?

999
01:15:16,513 --> 01:15:17,931
You were right all along.

1000
01:15:18,098 --> 01:15:21,226
<i>The decryption team at the VLA
has been working around the clock...</i>

1001
01:15:21,393 --> 01:15:24,104
<i>...and the hard work has finally paid off.</i>

1002
01:15:24,270 --> 01:15:28,233
<i>This key section, uncovered tonight
and obtained exclusively by CNN...</i>

1003
01:15:28,400 --> 01:15:31,027
<i>...shows an illustration
of what is of a human figure...</i>

1004
01:15:31,194 --> 01:15:34,030
<i>...inside a geometric structure
of some kind.</i>

1005
01:15:34,197 --> 01:15:36,032
<i>This and other contextual clues...</i>

1006
01:15:36,199 --> 01:15:39,285
<i>...now lead scientists to believe
that the message is, in fact...</i>

1007
01:15:39,452 --> 01:15:42,997
- President called an emergency meeting.
- Yeah, I imagine he would.

1008
01:15:43,164 --> 01:15:46,292
<i>...designed to take
a single human occupant into space...</i>

1009
01:15:46,459 --> 01:15:49,129
<i>...presumably to the star Vega.</i>

1010
01:16:07,647 --> 01:16:09,607
MAN: I'm gonna miss
my mother's birthday party.

1011
01:16:09,774 --> 01:16:12,652
KITZ: Call her in the morning,
tell her why you didn't show up.

1012
01:16:12,819 --> 01:16:14,320
Tell me.

1013
01:16:14,487 --> 01:16:15,613
They're gonna build it.

1014
01:16:15,780 --> 01:16:16,823
Who gets to go?

1015
01:16:16,990 --> 01:16:20,326
The president's just gone international
to spread the risk and cost...

1016
01:16:20,493 --> 01:16:22,036
Who gets to go, though?

1017
01:16:22,203 --> 01:16:23,830
Well, it's complicated, Ellie.

1018
01:16:24,748 --> 01:16:25,790
What?

1019
01:16:25,957 --> 01:16:28,793
They have formed a group
of international candidates.

1020
01:16:28,960 --> 01:16:31,004
Now, it's your discovery.

1021
01:16:31,421 --> 01:16:35,300
Okay, they understand that.
You're at the top of a very short list.

1022
01:16:35,467 --> 01:16:38,011
But they're also putting together
a selection committee.

1023
01:16:38,178 --> 01:16:42,348
It's made of scholars, theologians,
scientists, philosophers...

1024
01:16:42,515 --> 01:16:43,516
You're on it.

1025
01:16:44,726 --> 01:16:46,019
I've been asked, yes.

1026
01:16:48,980 --> 01:16:50,648
I guess that means...

1027
01:16:51,232 --> 01:16:53,985
...that you're a selector,
I'm a selectee and we can't...

1028
01:16:54,152 --> 01:16:55,487
Yeah.

1029
01:16:55,653 --> 01:16:58,072
What you don't understand, Ellie, is...

1030
01:16:58,239 --> 01:17:00,366
- I understand.
- Ellie...

1031
01:17:00,992 --> 01:17:05,330
I want you to know, whatever happens,
as far as I'm concerned...

1032
01:17:05,914 --> 01:17:07,415
Well...

1033
01:17:08,583 --> 01:17:10,168
...good luck.

1034
01:17:14,172 --> 01:17:17,634
Well, whatever you do, make sure
he doesn't monopolize the committee.

1035
01:17:17,801 --> 01:17:19,886
He is not on the committee, Ellie.

1036
01:17:21,638 --> 01:17:23,056
What?

1037
01:17:23,848 --> 01:17:26,893
He just resigned as science advisor.
He wants to be the one to go.

1038
01:17:29,103 --> 01:17:32,398
MAN [ON TV]: <i>The president is already
beginning to feel the financial pinch.</i>

1039
01:17:32,690 --> 01:17:34,776
<i>Sources say the cost of the project...</i>

1040
01:17:34,943 --> 01:17:38,571
<i>...is spiraling
to more than a third of a trillion dollars...</i>

1041
01:17:38,738 --> 01:17:42,325
<i>...making this what may be
the most expensive single project...</i>

1042
01:17:42,534 --> 01:17:44,410
<i>...in all of human history.</i>

1043
01:17:44,619 --> 01:17:47,956
<i>Meanwhile, months of debate finally
reached a conclusion last week...</i>

1044
01:17:48,122 --> 01:17:51,251
<i>...as 10 candidates
were selected by the IMC...</i>

1045
01:17:51,417 --> 01:17:55,380
<i>...representing a cross section of
political, philosophical and religious views.</i>

1046
01:17:55,588 --> 01:17:58,466
<i>Many of these are the same experts
who have been consulting...</i>

1047
01:17:58,633 --> 01:18:00,677
<i>...on the Machine's design and function.</i>

1048
01:18:00,885 --> 01:18:05,098
<i>They will be actively participating on-site
during the construction process.</i>

1049
01:18:05,265 --> 01:18:08,601
<i>But the choice of these 10 candidates
has not been without controversy.</i>

1050
01:18:08,768 --> 01:18:11,604
<i>Fully one-third of the candidates
are American.</i>

1051
01:18:11,771 --> 01:18:14,524
<i>Many international observers
are asking why.</i>

1052
01:18:14,691 --> 01:18:17,819
<i>The U.S. is shouldering an
enormous percentage of the financial...</i>

1053
01:18:18,027 --> 01:18:20,989
<i>Isn't it true that the contractors,
like Hadden Industries...</i>

1054
01:18:21,197 --> 01:18:24,534
<i>...are making those contributions
in exchange for technology rights?</i>

1055
01:18:24,742 --> 01:18:28,246
<i>Different nations are being compensated
in different ways. For example...</i>

1056
01:18:28,413 --> 01:18:32,959
<i>Japan made headlines when it announced it
would forego candidate representation...</i>

1057
01:18:33,126 --> 01:18:36,337
<i>...in exchange for the systems integration
subcontract.</i>

1058
01:18:36,504 --> 01:18:41,467
<i>On what basis do you choose
a human being to represent humanity?</i>

1059
01:18:41,676 --> 01:18:42,886
<i>Good question, Larry.</i>

1060
01:18:43,052 --> 01:18:45,013
<i>Yes, well, if nothing else...</i>

1061
01:18:45,179 --> 01:18:49,183
<i>...it should be somebody who is fluent in
the language the message was given in.</i>

1062
01:18:49,350 --> 01:18:50,560
<i>Science, in this case.</i>

1063
01:18:51,185 --> 01:18:53,605
<i>Doctor Arroway,
despite your obvious enthusiasm...</i>

1064
01:18:53,813 --> 01:18:56,024
<i>...there's been a lot of criticism of late...</i>

1065
01:18:56,274 --> 01:18:58,776
<i>...that this whole endeavor
is simply too dangerous.</i>

1066
01:18:58,943 --> 01:19:01,529
<i>Some of that criticism from scientists.</i>

1067
01:19:01,738 --> 01:19:04,532
<i>One Nobel Prize winner recently noted,
his words:</i>

1068
01:19:05,325 --> 01:19:07,035
<i>"There is every indication...</i>

1069
01:19:07,243 --> 01:19:11,039
<i>...that this is simply beyond our capabilities,
that this endeavor will fail...</i>

1070
01:19:11,247 --> 01:19:14,876
<i>...and that the Machine's occupant
will pay for that failure with their life."</i>

1071
01:19:15,043 --> 01:19:16,711
<i>How do you respond to that?</i>

1072
01:19:16,920 --> 01:19:20,298
ELLIE: <i>Well, this message is from
a civilization that may be anywhere...</i>

1073
01:19:20,506 --> 01:19:24,218
<i>...from hundreds to millions
and millions of years ahead of us.</i>

1074
01:19:24,469 --> 01:19:27,639
<i>I have to believe that
an intelligence that advanced...</i>

1075
01:19:28,264 --> 01:19:30,266
<i>...knows what they're doing.</i>

1076
01:19:31,184 --> 01:19:35,146
<i>Now all it requires on our part is...</i>

1077
01:19:35,313 --> 01:19:36,356
<i>Faith?</i>

1078
01:19:39,442 --> 01:19:41,527
<i>I was gonna say a sense of adventure.</i>

1079
01:19:41,736 --> 01:19:42,946
MAN:
<i>And in breaking news...</i>

1080
01:19:43,529 --> 01:19:44,822
<i>...a former astronaut...</i>

1081
01:19:45,031 --> 01:19:48,868
<i>...considered to be the leading American
candidate for the Machine seat...</i>

1082
01:19:49,077 --> 01:19:51,371
<i>...has unexpectedly dropped out
of the running.</i>

1083
01:19:51,788 --> 01:19:54,874
<i>Russell met with reporters this afternoon
at his Houston home.</i>

1084
01:19:55,208 --> 01:19:59,170
<i>I told my dad
that I didn't want him to go.</i>

1085
01:19:59,379 --> 01:20:01,089
<i>Daddy, don't go.</i>

1086
01:20:03,758 --> 01:20:06,844
<i>Nothing would give me more pride
than to...</i>

1087
01:20:07,512 --> 01:20:09,514
<i>...represent my country.</i>

1088
01:20:09,681 --> 01:20:11,891
<i>But what can I say to this?</i>

1089
01:20:12,475 --> 01:20:16,312
<i>Consortium officials had no comment,
but sources within the delegation...</i>

1090
01:20:16,479 --> 01:20:18,481
<i>...have disclosed that Dr. Arroway...</i>

1091
01:20:18,648 --> 01:20:22,735
<i>...appears to now be the front-runner
for the Machine seat.</i>

1092
01:20:23,569 --> 01:20:25,947
ELLIE:
"Meet me at noon. Must talk."

1093
01:20:26,114 --> 01:20:28,866
I didn't expect to be getting
a message like this from you.

1094
01:20:29,033 --> 01:20:30,243
Thanks for coming.

1095
01:20:30,451 --> 01:20:33,413
So is this kosher?
Fraternizing with the enemy?

1096
01:20:33,579 --> 01:20:37,083
Selectors and selectees mingling around?

1097
01:20:37,250 --> 01:20:39,419
Can we talk about Einstein?

1098
01:20:39,585 --> 01:20:41,754
Yeah, sure. Okay.

1099
01:20:41,921 --> 01:20:43,339
- Special relativity.
- Yeah.

1100
01:20:43,506 --> 01:20:45,133
This machine...

1101
01:20:45,717 --> 01:20:50,638
...now, if it works, and you travel to Vega
at even close to the speed of light...

1102
01:20:50,805 --> 01:20:53,307
- ...when you come back...
- If you come back.

1103
01:20:55,476 --> 01:20:57,478
If you come back...

1104
01:20:57,645 --> 01:21:01,941
...you'll only be 4 years older, but over
50 years would have passed here on Earth.

1105
01:21:02,108 --> 01:21:03,818
Basically, yeah.

1106
01:21:04,318 --> 01:21:06,904
And everybody that you care about...

1107
01:21:07,780 --> 01:21:10,116
...will be gone, dead and buried.

1108
01:21:10,950 --> 01:21:14,954
If you came back,
if you survived at all, which...

1109
01:21:16,289 --> 01:21:20,001
Look, Palmer, nobody's saying
this isn't dangerous, all right?

1110
01:21:20,168 --> 01:21:24,297
The rest of the candidates and myself,
we fully understand the risks we're taking.

1111
01:21:24,464 --> 01:21:25,715
Why?

1112
01:21:25,882 --> 01:21:28,593
Because it's a historic opportunity.
The world needs...

1113
01:21:28,801 --> 01:21:31,429
No, you, Ellie. You. You personally.

1114
01:21:32,472 --> 01:21:35,266
By doing this,
you're willing to give your life.

1115
01:21:35,475 --> 01:21:37,643
You're willing to die for it.

1116
01:21:39,479 --> 01:21:40,772
Why?

1117
01:21:44,484 --> 01:21:48,196
From as long as I can remember,
I've been searching for...

1118
01:21:48,821 --> 01:21:50,490
...something...

1119
01:21:50,698 --> 01:21:54,494
...some reason why we're here.
What are we doing here? Who are we?

1120
01:21:55,495 --> 01:21:59,373
If this is a chance to find out
even just a little part of that answer...

1121
01:22:00,875 --> 01:22:03,878
I don't know,
I think it's worth a human life.

1122
01:22:04,545 --> 01:22:06,089
Don't you?

1123
01:22:12,595 --> 01:22:15,223
You're an incredibly brave woman, Ellie.

1124
01:22:18,017 --> 01:22:20,144
Or incredibly nuts.

1125
01:22:42,208 --> 01:22:43,709
Palmer...

1126
01:22:49,423 --> 01:22:51,509
I'm really confused.

1127
01:22:52,802 --> 01:22:54,053
So am I.

1128
01:22:55,304 --> 01:22:58,891
I think I speak for us all
when I thank you for your patience.

1129
01:22:59,100 --> 01:23:02,562
Myself, I have one final question,
Dr. Arroway.

1130
01:23:04,063 --> 01:23:06,399
If you should meet these Vegans...

1131
01:23:06,607 --> 01:23:10,903
...and were permitted only
one question to ask of them...

1132
01:23:11,070 --> 01:23:12,738
...what would it be?

1133
01:23:14,198 --> 01:23:16,242
Well, I suppose it would be:

1134
01:23:16,409 --> 01:23:18,077
"How did you do it?

1135
01:23:18,619 --> 01:23:23,082
How did you evolve? How did you
survive this technological adolescence...

1136
01:23:23,249 --> 01:23:25,168
...without destroying yourself?"

1137
01:23:25,918 --> 01:23:30,798
<i>That more than any other question is one
I personally would like to have answered.</i>

1138
01:23:31,132 --> 01:23:32,967
Not too bad, Mike.

1139
01:23:33,634 --> 01:23:34,927
PATEL:
<i>I join Dr. Bergeron...</i>

1140
01:23:35,094 --> 01:23:38,556
...in expressing thanks for
your patience, Dr. Arroway.

1141
01:23:38,764 --> 01:23:41,267
We've all been most impressed
with your candor...

1142
01:23:41,434 --> 01:23:43,477
...and your stamina.

1143
01:23:43,644 --> 01:23:46,814
I believe we are ready to move on
to your final statement.

1144
01:23:46,981 --> 01:23:48,900
Madam Chairman,
I have one more question.

1145
01:23:49,066 --> 01:23:50,818
Yes, Mr. Joss.

1146
01:23:51,444 --> 01:23:53,446
Dr. Arroway...

1147
01:23:54,822 --> 01:23:57,158
Would you consider yourself...

1148
01:23:57,658 --> 01:24:00,286
...a spiritual person?

1149
01:24:02,955 --> 01:24:04,707
Uh...

1150
01:24:05,458 --> 01:24:08,794
I don't really understand
the point of the question.

1151
01:24:08,961 --> 01:24:11,839
I consider myself a moral person.

1152
01:24:12,006 --> 01:24:13,966
PATEL:
I'm sure we all agree that is the case...

1153
01:24:14,133 --> 01:24:16,636
...but I think what Mr. Joss
is in fact asking...

1154
01:24:16,802 --> 01:24:18,638
Do you believe in God...

1155
01:24:20,640 --> 01:24:22,642
...Dr. Arroway?

1156
01:24:27,146 --> 01:24:29,565
As a scientist...

1157
01:24:29,732 --> 01:24:33,194
...I rely on empirical evidence,
and in this matter...

1158
01:24:33,361 --> 01:24:37,073
...I don't believe
that there is data either way.

1159
01:24:37,657 --> 01:24:42,078
PATEL: So your answer would in fact be
that you don't believe in God.

1160
01:24:45,498 --> 01:24:48,542
I don't understand the relevance
of the question.

1161
01:24:49,252 --> 01:24:54,382
Dr. Arroway,
95 percent of the world's population...

1162
01:24:54,548 --> 01:24:58,511
...believes in a supreme being
in one form or another.

1163
01:24:59,679 --> 01:25:02,890
I believe that makes the question
more than relevant.

1164
01:25:05,559 --> 01:25:07,270
PATEL:
Dr. Arroway?

1165
01:25:17,196 --> 01:25:19,198
I believe, um...

1166
01:25:23,202 --> 01:25:25,705
I believe I've already
answered that question.

1167
01:25:29,750 --> 01:25:33,254
<i>I'm proud of what
we've achieved as a species...</i>

1168
01:25:33,421 --> 01:25:35,423
<i>...and as a civilization.</i>

1169
01:25:37,091 --> 01:25:39,593
<i>I would hate to see
everything we stand for...</i>

1170
01:25:39,760 --> 01:25:43,681
<i>...all that we have fought for
for a thousand generations...</i>

1171
01:25:43,889 --> 01:25:46,225
<i>...all that God has blessed us with...</i>

1172
01:25:47,560 --> 01:25:49,103
...betrayed...

1173
01:25:50,062 --> 01:25:54,650
...in the final hour because we chose to
send a representative who did not put our...

1174
01:25:55,151 --> 01:25:57,945
<i>...most cherished beliefs first.</i>

1175
01:26:01,240 --> 01:26:02,408
<i>Thank you.</i>

1176
01:26:08,581 --> 01:26:10,916
[KNOCKING]

1177
01:26:35,649 --> 01:26:36,942
Why did you do it?

1178
01:26:41,530 --> 01:26:45,701
Our job was to select someone
to speak for everybody.

1179
01:26:48,996 --> 01:26:52,458
I just couldn't in good conscience
vote for a person...

1180
01:26:52,625 --> 01:26:54,627
...who doesn't believe in God.

1181
01:26:56,670 --> 01:26:58,047
Someone who...

1182
01:26:58,214 --> 01:27:01,634
...honestly thinks
the other 95 percent of us...

1183
01:27:01,842 --> 01:27:04,804
...suffer from some form
of mass delusion.

1184
01:27:07,973 --> 01:27:10,059
I told the truth up there.

1185
01:27:11,352 --> 01:27:14,855
And Drumlin told you exactly
what you wanted to hear.

1186
01:27:50,015 --> 01:27:52,601
DRUMLIN:
<i>With full humility and utmost gratitude...</i>

1187
01:27:52,768 --> 01:27:54,979
<i>...I accept this great honor...</i>

1188
01:27:55,187 --> 01:27:59,233
<i>...and I promise I will do my best
to represent my nation...</i>

1189
01:27:59,400 --> 01:28:04,530
<i>...my planet and my God
on this historic journey.</i>

1190
01:28:04,697 --> 01:28:07,032
<i>I thank you from the bottom of my heart.</i>

1191
01:28:07,199 --> 01:28:10,161
[APPLAUDING]

1192
01:28:49,617 --> 01:28:52,578
MAN [ON TV]: <i>There's
a carnival atmosphere at Cape Canaveral...</i>

1193
01:28:52,745 --> 01:28:54,788
<i>...as the International
Machine Consortium...</i>

1194
01:28:54,955 --> 01:28:58,417
<i>...hosts as much a media event
as a systems test.</i>

1195
01:28:58,918 --> 01:29:02,588
<i>David Drumlin is supervising
the on-site portion of the test.</i>

1196
01:29:02,796 --> 01:29:04,590
<i>We spoke with him earlier today.</i>

1197
01:29:04,757 --> 01:29:07,259
DRUMLIN: <i>We'll be able to monitor
the stresses and effects...</i>

1198
01:29:07,426 --> 01:29:09,762
<i>...using the robot passenger...</i>

1199
01:29:10,262 --> 01:29:12,556
<i>...who we lovingly call Elmer.</i>

1200
01:29:12,723 --> 01:29:15,851
WOMAN [OVER SPEAKER]: <i>We are coming up
on T-minus two hours and counting.</i>

1201
01:29:16,018 --> 01:29:18,062
<i>All Machine systems are go.</i>

1202
01:29:18,229 --> 01:29:21,106
Dr. Arroway, the press would like to
ask you a few questions...

1203
01:29:21,273 --> 01:29:23,943
...as soon as they're done
with Dr. Drumlin.

1204
01:29:25,236 --> 01:29:27,488
DRUMLIN: Now, at the moment
where we release the pod...

1205
01:29:27,655 --> 01:29:31,242
...there will be a substantial
amount of energy generated at the core.

1206
01:29:31,742 --> 01:29:34,119
We'll be able to monitor
the stresses and effects...

1207
01:29:34,286 --> 01:29:38,457
...using our robot passenger,
who we lovingly call Elmer.

1208
01:29:38,624 --> 01:29:39,792
[CHUCKLES]

1209
01:29:39,959 --> 01:29:41,585
- Thank you.
- Welcome.

1210
01:29:41,752 --> 01:29:43,671
WOMAN:
<i>...the test area is clear and go.</i>

1211
01:29:43,837 --> 01:29:46,632
Sir, they're ready for you out at the site.

1212
01:29:46,799 --> 01:29:48,425
Yeah, just a minute.

1213
01:29:49,468 --> 01:29:52,763
WOMAN: <i>The test director has issued a go
to enter the final countdown phase.</i>

1214
01:29:52,930 --> 01:29:54,265
Ellie.

1215
01:29:54,431 --> 01:29:56,809
- Congratulations, David.
- Glad you could come.

1216
01:29:58,143 --> 01:30:00,604
Didn't, uh, expect to see you here.

1217
01:30:01,313 --> 01:30:06,151
Well, I'm still, you know,
Crew Ops at the Control Center.

1218
01:30:06,819 --> 01:30:10,823
And I suppose the fact that I discovered
the message means that I have PR value.

1219
01:30:10,990 --> 01:30:12,658
Of course.

1220
01:30:14,159 --> 01:30:17,329
Ellie, I know you must think
this is all very unfair.

1221
01:30:17,997 --> 01:30:20,291
And maybe that's an understatement.

1222
01:30:21,375 --> 01:30:23,752
What you don't know is, I agree.

1223
01:30:24,336 --> 01:30:27,631
I wish the world was a place
where fair was the bottom line.

1224
01:30:27,798 --> 01:30:31,010
Where the kind of idealism you showed
at the hearing was rewarded...

1225
01:30:31,176 --> 01:30:33,470
...not taken advantage of.

1226
01:30:35,180 --> 01:30:38,309
Unfortunately,
we don't live in that world.

1227
01:30:40,352 --> 01:30:41,854
Funny...

1228
01:30:42,896 --> 01:30:46,358
...I always believed that the world
is what we make of it.

1229
01:30:48,027 --> 01:30:51,196
Excuse me, Dr. Drumlin, sir?

1230
01:30:52,823 --> 01:30:54,366
Good luck on the test.

1231
01:30:54,617 --> 01:30:56,952
<i>- The pad leader has issued the order...</i>
- Thank you.

1232
01:30:57,119 --> 01:31:00,289
<i>- ...to clear the pad of all nonessential...</i>
- Right this way, sir.

1233
01:31:04,793 --> 01:31:08,672
<i>Test director, everything looks good
up here. We're ready to go.</i>

1234
01:31:08,839 --> 01:31:11,842
STEVE: All right, people, I want
things double and triple checked here.

1235
01:31:12,009 --> 01:31:15,596
We only get one shot at this.
Let's get it right.

1236
01:31:15,763 --> 01:31:18,849
Ladies and gentlemen,
we are ready for startup.

1237
01:31:19,058 --> 01:31:22,394
All controllers, I need a go-no-go
for test initiation.

1238
01:31:22,561 --> 01:31:23,562
-Mechanical?
MAN 1: Go.

1239
01:31:23,729 --> 01:31:24,772
-Electrical?
MAN 2: Go.

1240
01:31:24,938 --> 01:31:26,023
-Dynamics?
MAN 3: Go.

1241
01:31:26,190 --> 01:31:27,483
-Communications?
MAN 4: Go.

1242
01:31:27,650 --> 01:31:28,692
-Pod systems?
WOMAN: Go

1243
01:31:28,859 --> 01:31:30,402
-Safety?
MAN 5: Go.

1244
01:31:30,569 --> 01:31:33,489
Crew Ops, verify all pre-test
procedures are complete.

1245
01:31:33,656 --> 01:31:35,074
Yes, verified. I'm go.

1246
01:31:35,240 --> 01:31:37,743
Pad leader, we are good.
Is everything okay with you?

1247
01:31:37,910 --> 01:31:40,579
<i>Roger. The pad is a go.</i>

1248
01:31:40,746 --> 01:31:43,582
STEVE:
Test initiation, on my mark.

1249
01:31:44,041 --> 01:31:45,417
Five...

1250
01:31:45,584 --> 01:31:46,835
...four...

1251
01:31:47,002 --> 01:31:48,295
...three...

1252
01:31:48,462 --> 01:31:49,672
...two...

1253
01:31:50,089 --> 01:31:51,590
...one.

1254
01:31:51,757 --> 01:31:53,175
Mark.

1255
01:31:53,342 --> 01:31:55,094
MAN 1: Test start.
MAN 2: Rings are unlocked.

1256
01:31:55,260 --> 01:31:57,012
MAN 3:
Power levels indicate a good start.

1257
01:31:57,179 --> 01:32:00,057
WOMAN 1: <i>We have begun the full-scale
test of Earth's first system...</i>

1258
01:32:00,224 --> 01:32:03,769
<i>...built by the sharing of knowledge
with a neighbor in our universe.</i>

1259
01:32:03,936 --> 01:32:06,188
MAN 4:
All power sources on core induction bus.

1260
01:32:06,355 --> 01:32:07,981
WOMAN 2:
IPV power transfer.

1261
01:32:08,148 --> 01:32:11,902
GERRY: Ring motion detect.
Alpha, Bravo, Charlie.

1262
01:32:12,945 --> 01:32:15,989
STEVE: Gerry, how are we looking?
GERRY: Looks good. We're underway.

1263
01:32:16,156 --> 01:32:17,908
DRUMLIN:
<i>Looks like we have a normal startup.</i>

1264
01:32:18,075 --> 01:32:20,619
STEVE:
Roger, David. It's looking good here.

1265
01:32:20,786 --> 01:32:23,038
[CHEERING]

1266
01:32:26,250 --> 01:32:29,378
WOMAN 1: <i>We are moments away from
the initiation of the drop sequence.</i>

1267
01:32:29,545 --> 01:32:31,547
<i>All systems are go.</i>

1268
01:32:33,674 --> 01:32:36,301
STEVE: Mechanical, you still happy?
MAN 1: Roger that.

1269
01:32:36,468 --> 01:32:39,930
DRUMLIN: <i>I'll tell you, this is very exciting
from up here.</i>

1270
01:32:42,099 --> 01:32:45,728
GERRY: We've got 4 percent.
DRUMLIN: <i>We're confirming 4 percent.</i>

1271
01:32:46,979 --> 01:32:50,315
<i>Frequency 9.85.</i>

1272
01:32:50,482 --> 01:32:56,238
<i>Harmonics in the green. G-level 1.1.</i>

1273
01:32:56,405 --> 01:32:59,742
- Five percent.
- Confirmed. We are at 5 percent.

1274
01:32:59,908 --> 01:33:01,660
David, how's it looking to you?

1275
01:33:01,827 --> 01:33:04,663
DRUMLIN: <i>Okay. I'm gonna take a look
and show you what we got.</i>

1276
01:33:04,830 --> 01:33:06,999
MAN 2: <i>All test-subject instrumentation
looks good.</i>

1277
01:33:07,166 --> 01:33:10,836
Confirmed, we have a good picture
from Dr. Drumlin's PRD.

1278
01:33:11,003 --> 01:33:13,464
DRUMLIN: <i>Hope you guys are seeing
what I'm seeing.</i>

1279
01:33:13,839 --> 01:33:15,758
MAN 3:
<i>All systems are go.</i>

1280
01:33:17,342 --> 01:33:18,927
DRUMLIN:
<i>This is really something.</i>

1281
01:33:19,136 --> 01:33:20,804
MAN 4:
<i>For purposes of this test...</i>

1282
01:33:20,971 --> 01:33:25,184
<i>...Dr. Drumlin will be on top of
the gantry crane that's over the Machine.</i>

1283
01:33:25,350 --> 01:33:27,519
<i>We've got some video animation...</i>

1284
01:33:27,686 --> 01:33:31,190
<i>...to show you just how this machine,
it is thought, will work.</i>

1285
01:33:31,356 --> 01:33:33,025
<i>The passenger pod will be released.</i>

1286
01:33:33,233 --> 01:33:37,571
<i>It will come down through the center of
the rings and into the Machine's core.</i>

1287
01:33:37,738 --> 01:33:41,825
<i>The big question everyone here is asking is,
of course, what happens then?</i>

1288
01:33:41,992 --> 01:33:46,830
<i>Some believe that the rings function as
some sort of an accelerator or something...</i>

1289
01:33:46,997 --> 01:33:49,625
<i>...sending its energy
into this passenger pod...</i>

1290
01:33:49,792 --> 01:33:52,503
<i>...making it rocket away
at near the speed of light.</i>

1291
01:33:52,711 --> 01:33:57,341
<i>Other people believe it might open up
a doorway to some other dimension.</i>

1292
01:33:57,508 --> 01:34:00,969
<i>Who's right?
Well, that's what today's test is all about.</i>

1293
01:34:01,595 --> 01:34:04,056
GERRY: Thirty-five percent.
MAN 1: Subsystems, are you ready?

1294
01:34:04,223 --> 01:34:07,392
GERRY: Forty percent normal acceleration.
MAN 2: <i>Roger, confirm 40 percent.</i>

1295
01:34:07,601 --> 01:34:10,562
STEVE: All controllers stand by
to initiate drop sequence...

1296
01:34:10,729 --> 01:34:12,314
...on my mark.

1297
01:34:12,481 --> 01:34:14,817
MAN 3:
<i>All systems ready for drop sequence.</i>

1298
01:34:14,983 --> 01:34:17,236
GERRY:
Forty-five percent.

1299
01:34:17,402 --> 01:34:19,571
DRUMLIN:
<i>We're reading 45 percent.</i>

1300
01:34:20,572 --> 01:34:22,574
<i>Can you verify closure?</i>

1301
01:34:23,867 --> 01:34:25,410
GERRY:
Forty-six.

1302
01:34:25,577 --> 01:34:30,249
STEVE: All controllers stand by
to initiate IPV drop sequence. On my mark.

1303
01:34:30,916 --> 01:34:32,668
GERRY:
Forty-eight percent.

1304
01:34:33,293 --> 01:34:35,712
DRUMLIN: <i>Test director,
I'm feeling a very strange vibration.</i>

1305
01:34:35,879 --> 01:34:37,089
<i>Are you feeling it down there?</i>

1306
01:34:37,256 --> 01:34:39,216
STEVE:
Gerry, can you confirm any vibration?

1307
01:34:39,758 --> 01:34:41,593
GERRY:
Negative, everything looks good here.

1308
01:34:41,760 --> 01:34:43,762
- Crew Ops?
- Nothing wrong with Elmer.

1309
01:34:43,929 --> 01:34:45,764
We're not seeing anything abnormal.

1310
01:34:45,931 --> 01:34:48,725
DRUMLIN: <i>No good. Hold the sequence.
I'm gonna take a direct reading.</i>

1311
01:34:49,226 --> 01:34:52,604
STEVE: What the hell is he talking about?
- Can anyone say grandstanding?

1312
01:34:52,771 --> 01:34:54,773
He sure knows he's on TV!

1313
01:34:54,940 --> 01:34:58,068
Back home that's what we call
an overcooked ham.

1314
01:34:58,235 --> 01:35:00,737
DRUMLIN:
<i>I'm reading 10 kilohertz!</i>

1315
01:35:00,904 --> 01:35:03,699
<i>What's your reading on the first
and second order G-levels?</i>

1316
01:35:03,907 --> 01:35:05,784
MAN 1:
G-levels are right on.

1317
01:35:06,285 --> 01:35:09,788
MAN 2: <i>We confirm Drumlin's readings
and they're all normal. We're go.</i>

1318
01:35:09,955 --> 01:35:11,456
STEVE:
Copy that, Dynamics.

1319
01:35:11,623 --> 01:35:14,376
I think we're okay,
but we'll let him finish.

1320
01:35:15,586 --> 01:35:17,629
-Mechanical, you still okay?
MAN 3: Roger, chief.

1321
01:35:17,796 --> 01:35:19,131
We're right on the money.

1322
01:35:19,298 --> 01:35:21,592
We've got a security problem here.

1323
01:35:21,758 --> 01:35:23,343
Ellie, are you sure?

1324
01:35:23,510 --> 01:35:25,804
Yeah. This guy, he shouldn't be there.
I know him.

1325
01:35:25,971 --> 01:35:28,932
Gerry, execute the
rapid shutdown sequence.

1326
01:35:29,099 --> 01:35:32,394
Security, move emergency
response team into position one.

1327
01:35:32,561 --> 01:35:35,147
- Get me Drumlin on a secure loop, please.
- You got him.

1328
01:35:35,314 --> 01:35:38,817
-David, can you hear me?
DRUMLIN: <i>Yes, I hear.</i>

1329
01:35:38,984 --> 01:35:41,278
ELLIE: All right, we've got
a security breach here.

1330
01:35:41,653 --> 01:35:43,655
Right behind you. The tall guy.

1331
01:35:44,156 --> 01:35:47,409
The technician. See him?

1332
01:35:49,453 --> 01:35:51,830
He's not supposed to be there.

1333
01:35:56,501 --> 01:35:59,171
David, he's got something in his hand!

1334
01:35:59,338 --> 01:36:01,882
<i>Him! Take him! Security breach!</i>

1335
01:36:02,549 --> 01:36:03,675
He's got a bomb!

1336
01:36:03,842 --> 01:36:06,470
STEVE: I want that emergency-response
team up on the gantry now.

1337
01:36:06,637 --> 01:36:08,305
Security, stage one alert.

1338
01:36:08,472 --> 01:36:12,976
By God, move all the fire and
rescue personnel into position one.

1339
01:36:13,810 --> 01:36:15,646
[YELLING]

1340
01:36:28,992 --> 01:36:31,536
[SCREAMING]

1341
01:36:49,554 --> 01:36:51,682
STEVE:
All right, all right, back to your stations.

1342
01:36:51,848 --> 01:36:54,977
I need you to be professional, people.
Settle down.

1343
01:36:55,185 --> 01:36:58,063
Pad leader, pad leader, do you copy?

1344
01:37:15,497 --> 01:37:18,166
<i>The scene earlier today,
when David Drumlin...</i>

1345
01:37:18,375 --> 01:37:20,085
<i>...advisor to two presidents...</i>

1346
01:37:20,252 --> 01:37:24,256
<i>...and winner of the National Academy
of Sciences lifetime award...</i>

1347
01:37:24,423 --> 01:37:28,218
<i>...was laid to rest
at Arlington National Cemetery.</i>

1348
01:37:31,930 --> 01:37:33,849
<i>In a related story, last night's raid...</i>

1349
01:37:34,016 --> 01:37:35,559
Can I help you?

1350
01:37:35,726 --> 01:37:39,771
- Oh, you're... I've got some mail for you.
<i>- ...resulted in the discovery of this video.</i>

1351
01:37:39,938 --> 01:37:44,651
<i>The suicide note of the leader of the
terrorist group now believed responsible...</i>

1352
01:37:44,818 --> 01:37:47,362
<i>...for the explosion in Florida this week.</i>

1353
01:37:47,529 --> 01:37:52,534
<i>What we do,
we do for the goodness of all mankind.</i>

1354
01:37:53,201 --> 01:37:56,121
<i>This won't be understood, not now...</i>

1355
01:37:56,288 --> 01:37:58,248
<i>...but the apocalypse to come...</i>

1356
01:37:59,416 --> 01:38:01,585
<i>...will vindicate our faith.</i>

1357
01:38:10,719 --> 01:38:14,431
[PULSING]

1358
01:38:25,901 --> 01:38:27,736
Welcome home.

1359
01:39:23,333 --> 01:39:25,627
[MAN SPEAKING IN RUSSIAN
ON TV]

1360
01:39:33,510 --> 01:39:35,887
<i>Oh, Comrade Arroway...</i>

1361
01:39:36,054 --> 01:39:39,349
<i>...he has been expecting your call.
One moment, please.</i>

1362
01:39:49,109 --> 01:39:50,318
Mr. Hadden?

1363
01:39:50,944 --> 01:39:52,279
<i>Doctor.</i>

1364
01:39:53,405 --> 01:39:55,198
<i>How kind of you to call.</i>

1365
01:39:55,907 --> 01:39:58,326
Mr. Hadden, where are you?

1366
01:40:00,036 --> 01:40:04,708
<i>The Russian government was kind enough
to give me accommodations on Mir.</i>

1367
01:40:05,542 --> 01:40:07,419
You're living on a space station?

1368
01:40:08,920 --> 01:40:11,256
<i>It's quite simple, really.</i>

1369
01:40:12,299 --> 01:40:15,886
<i>The low-oxygen,
zero-gravity environment...</i>

1370
01:40:16,052 --> 01:40:20,765
<i>...is the only thing keeping
the cancer from eating me alive.</i>

1371
01:40:22,309 --> 01:40:25,020
<i>Actually, I quite like it up here.</i>

1372
01:40:25,478 --> 01:40:29,274
<i>My little room has one hell of a view.</i>

1373
01:40:36,448 --> 01:40:38,950
<i>I wanna show you something.</i>

1374
01:40:43,121 --> 01:40:45,582
<i>Hokkaido Island.</i>

1375
01:40:46,499 --> 01:40:48,877
The systems integration site.

1376
01:40:49,878 --> 01:40:52,130
<i>Look closer.</i>

1377
01:40:59,763 --> 01:41:03,391
<i>First rule in government spending:</i>

1378
01:41:03,558 --> 01:41:07,479
<i>Why build one when you can have
two at twice the price?</i>

1379
01:41:07,646 --> 01:41:11,441
<i>Only, this one can be kept secret.</i>

1380
01:41:11,608 --> 01:41:13,652
<i>Controlled by Americans...</i>

1381
01:41:13,818 --> 01:41:17,447
<i>...built by the Japanese subcontractors...</i>

1382
01:41:18,156 --> 01:41:21,743
<i>...who also happen to be...</i>

1383
01:41:21,910 --> 01:41:23,995
<i>...recently acquired...</i>

1384
01:41:24,162 --> 01:41:27,290
<i>...wholly-owned subsidiaries...</i>

1385
01:41:27,457 --> 01:41:29,834
<i>- ...of Hadden Industries.</i>
- Of Hadden Industries.

1386
01:41:33,004 --> 01:41:36,174
<i>They still want
an American to go, doctor.</i>

1387
01:41:39,803 --> 01:41:42,138
<i>Wanna take a ride?</i>

1388
01:42:24,264 --> 01:42:28,560
WOMAN: And augmenting our restraint and
communications console subsystem...

1389
01:42:28,727 --> 01:42:30,729
...we have your personal recording unit.

1390
01:42:30,895 --> 01:42:35,025
Normal, infrared and ultraviolet lenses.

1391
01:42:35,191 --> 01:42:39,237
Digital microchip good for
thousands of hours of recording.

1392
01:42:40,447 --> 01:42:42,532
Okay, look, I understand...

1393
01:42:42,699 --> 01:42:46,119
...the reason for recording
and documenting the mission.

1394
01:42:46,286 --> 01:42:49,122
But I just... I wanna go on record
one more time.

1395
01:42:49,289 --> 01:42:52,792
The transmitted specs
never said anything about any chair...

1396
01:42:53,043 --> 01:42:57,547
...or a restraining harness or survival gear.

1397
01:42:57,714 --> 01:42:59,549
Why can't we just trust the original...?

1398
01:42:59,716 --> 01:43:03,845
Doctor, both the IMC Board
and the SI team reviewed the matter...

1399
01:43:04,054 --> 01:43:07,807
...and concluded that
the design impact is negligible.

1400
01:43:07,974 --> 01:43:09,684
Bottom line is...

1401
01:43:09,851 --> 01:43:12,187
...we're not putting anybody
aboard this machine...

1402
01:43:12,395 --> 01:43:15,398
...unless there is
some sort of minimal protection.

1403
01:43:15,565 --> 01:43:17,442
End of story.

1404
01:43:19,861 --> 01:43:23,114
-Okay.
MAN: Ellie...

1405
01:43:23,990 --> 01:43:28,078
...we've been giving these to the astronauts
since the beginning of the space program.

1406
01:43:29,412 --> 01:43:31,706
It's never been made public, of course.

1407
01:43:31,915 --> 01:43:34,000
It's fast and it's painless.

1408
01:43:36,753 --> 01:43:39,631
I'm gonna travel 26 light years
to commit suicide?

1409
01:43:39,798 --> 01:43:42,008
Something may go wrong.

1410
01:43:42,175 --> 01:43:44,928
There may be
an unforeseen mechanical failure.

1411
01:43:45,678 --> 01:43:48,765
You could be marooned, unable to return.

1412
01:43:49,933 --> 01:43:53,061
There are a thousand reasons
we can think of for you to have this...

1413
01:43:53,269 --> 01:43:56,439
...but mostly it's for the reasons
we can't think of.

1414
01:44:21,506 --> 01:44:22,549
[KNOCKING]

1415
01:44:22,715 --> 01:44:23,842
Come in.

1416
01:44:25,385 --> 01:44:29,389
- Dr. Arroway, time to get ready.
- Thank you.

1417
01:44:40,066 --> 01:44:41,776
I know you don't have much time.

1418
01:44:41,943 --> 01:44:45,029
As soon as I found out about the Machine,
I called the president.

1419
01:44:45,238 --> 01:44:47,323
I came with Kitz.

1420
01:44:47,490 --> 01:44:49,784
I had to see you one more time.

1421
01:44:49,951 --> 01:44:51,703
- Oh, God, I'm sorry.
- It's okay.

1422
01:44:51,870 --> 01:44:53,746
No, I'm glad you came.

1423
01:44:53,913 --> 01:44:56,791
There's something I gotta say to you.

1424
01:44:57,667 --> 01:45:01,921
The reason I didn't vote for you to go was
a good reason, but it wasn't the real one.

1425
01:45:03,006 --> 01:45:05,008
I didn't vote for you to go...

1426
01:45:06,551 --> 01:45:08,720
...because I don't wanna lose you.

1427
01:45:09,762 --> 01:45:12,599
You find your way home, all right?

1428
01:45:28,990 --> 01:45:30,408
<i>Twenty-three hours ago...</i>

1429
01:45:30,575 --> 01:45:34,621
<i>...the world was stunned by the
announcement of a second Machine...</i>

1430
01:45:34,787 --> 01:45:37,916
<i>...and its passenger,
Eleanor Ann Arroway.</i>

1431
01:45:38,124 --> 01:45:41,711
<i>Now the question
that's on everyone's mind:</i>

1432
01:45:41,878 --> 01:45:43,880
<i>Will history repeat itself?</i>

1433
01:45:44,047 --> 01:45:46,341
<i>With a piece of engineering
this complex...</i>

1434
01:45:46,508 --> 01:45:50,595
<i>...with so many unknowns,
could the unthinkable occur?</i>

1435
01:45:50,762 --> 01:45:54,224
<i>The security in Hokkaido
has been incredibly tight.</i>

1436
01:45:54,390 --> 01:45:57,727
<i>No reporters whatsoever
have been allowed near the site.</i>

1437
01:48:19,702 --> 01:48:21,704
Thanks.

1438
01:48:38,262 --> 01:48:41,265
Magnetic restraint is secure.
All life support functions normal.

1439
01:48:41,474 --> 01:48:44,644
Roger that. Ellie, this is Control.
Do you copy?

1440
01:48:44,811 --> 01:48:47,146
Ellie to Control, reading you five-by-five.

1441
01:48:47,313 --> 01:48:51,401
- Comm 1, Give me Ellie's PRD.
<i>- Video unit recording and transmitting.</i>

1442
01:48:52,360 --> 01:48:54,904
<i>-Are you reading me?</i>
STEVE: That's affirmative.

1443
01:48:55,071 --> 01:48:57,365
Video looks good.

1444
01:48:57,532 --> 01:49:00,076
<i>- Copy that, Control.</i>
- Okay.

1445
01:49:00,243 --> 01:49:03,121
Ellie, we're gonna proceed
to button you up.

1446
01:49:03,287 --> 01:49:05,957
<i>Roger, Control. I'm ready.</i>

1447
01:49:09,377 --> 01:49:11,754
- Dynamics...
<i>- I can confirm the walkway retraction.</i>

1448
01:49:11,921 --> 01:49:12,922
STEVE: Systems?
MAN 1: Go.

1449
01:49:13,089 --> 01:49:14,465
STEVE: Communications?
MAN 2: Go.

1450
01:49:14,632 --> 01:49:16,300
STEVE: Life support?
WOMAN: Go.

1451
01:49:16,467 --> 01:49:18,761
Ellie, we are go for closure.

1452
01:49:20,221 --> 01:49:22,098
<i>I'm go here.</i>

1453
01:49:22,974 --> 01:49:26,477
STEVE: Mechanical, initiate
IPV closure sequence.

1454
01:49:28,104 --> 01:49:30,314
Hold on, let me get you a chair.

1455
01:49:31,023 --> 01:49:32,066
ELLIE:
<i>I'm go.</i>

1456
01:49:32,233 --> 01:49:36,112
- It's nice to smell you again, Mr. Kitz.
- You too.

1457
01:49:36,279 --> 01:49:38,114
Wouldn't peg him as a Polo man.

1458
01:49:47,665 --> 01:49:49,000
I got a visual on the door.

1459
01:49:49,167 --> 01:49:51,127
WOMAN:
IPV escape system is armed.

1460
01:49:51,294 --> 01:49:53,296
Door is looking good.

1461
01:49:58,509 --> 01:50:00,178
Door alignment looks good.

1462
01:50:28,790 --> 01:50:30,875
Ellie, we are showing green
across the board.

1463
01:50:31,042 --> 01:50:34,378
Copy that, Control. IPV is secure.

1464
01:50:41,511 --> 01:50:43,513
GERRY:
<i>We are at 30 percent.</i>

1465
01:50:43,679 --> 01:50:44,972
Copy that, 30 percent.

1466
01:50:49,852 --> 01:50:52,522
- How you doing in there, EI?
<i>- Kent?</i>

1467
01:50:53,272 --> 01:50:56,067
- Is that you?
<i>- That's a big affirmative.</i>

1468
01:50:56,984 --> 01:50:58,569
Who let you in there, huh?

1469
01:50:58,736 --> 01:51:00,863
<i>A higher power intervened.</i>

1470
01:51:02,907 --> 01:51:04,242
Well, I'm glad you came.

1471
01:51:08,996 --> 01:51:10,164
GERRY:
Thirty-five percent.

1472
01:51:10,915 --> 01:51:12,416
Copy that, 35 percent.

1473
01:51:12,583 --> 01:51:13,918
- Mechanical?
- We're good.

1474
01:51:14,085 --> 01:51:15,419
Forty percent.

1475
01:51:19,048 --> 01:51:20,925
I'm picking up a moderate vibration here.

1476
01:51:22,093 --> 01:51:25,721
-Dynamics, can you confirm vibration?
GERRY: Everything looks good here.

1477
01:51:25,930 --> 01:51:29,183
She's feeling the same thing
they felt at the Cape at this point.

1478
01:51:29,350 --> 01:51:30,935
Ellie, the vibration is normal.

1479
01:51:31,102 --> 01:51:33,604
GERRY: <i>We are at 50 percent.
All systems nominal.</i>

1480
01:51:33,771 --> 01:51:35,773
Copy that, 50 percent.

1481
01:51:40,945 --> 01:51:42,947
Vibration is getting a little stronger now.

1482
01:51:43,114 --> 01:51:45,950
We're picking up
some low-frequency noise.

1483
01:51:46,325 --> 01:51:49,412
- Mechanical, can you confirm?
- It looks fine to me.

1484
01:51:49,579 --> 01:51:52,957
STEVE: Roger. Gerry, what do you think?
- I'm still go.

1485
01:51:53,124 --> 01:51:54,750
People, we are still go.

1486
01:51:56,502 --> 01:51:58,963
What...? Something's happening.

1487
01:52:00,214 --> 01:52:02,884
I'm... There's...

1488
01:52:03,050 --> 01:52:05,887
There's a light. Do you see that?
I'm seeing some...

1489
01:52:06,637 --> 01:52:09,098
<i>Something, some...</i>

1490
01:52:09,265 --> 01:52:11,809
I can't tell whether it's daylight or not.

1491
01:52:11,976 --> 01:52:14,896
There it is again.
It's coming from the bottom.

1492
01:52:15,938 --> 01:52:17,481
Here, let me show you. See that?

1493
01:52:17,648 --> 01:52:19,567
Negative. Ellie, you're breaking up.

1494
01:52:19,734 --> 01:52:22,570
- Comm 1, can we boost her signal?
- Already at maximum.

1495
01:52:22,737 --> 01:52:24,280
GERRY:
Sixty-five percent.

1496
01:52:24,447 --> 01:52:26,157
The material is changing.

1497
01:52:26,324 --> 01:52:29,827
It's bordering on translucence.
But the system is intact.

1498
01:52:33,122 --> 01:52:37,501
It's gotta be some kind
of electromagnetic field.

1499
01:52:37,668 --> 01:52:40,671
<i>- Are you reading this?</i>
- Negative, we're seeing only interference.

1500
01:52:40,838 --> 01:52:46,177
It's becoming completely translucent.
I can't describe it. I can't even explain it.

1501
01:52:48,638 --> 01:52:49,680
Seventy percent.

1502
01:52:49,847 --> 01:52:52,183
People, we have intermittent comm,
but we are go.

1503
01:52:52,350 --> 01:52:56,812
-Initiate secondary drive systems.
GERRY: Initiating auxiliary booster sequence.

1504
01:52:57,021 --> 01:52:58,856
MAN:
We're go for ignition.

1505
01:53:05,905 --> 01:53:08,366
We have auxiliary booster ignition.

1506
01:53:14,038 --> 01:53:15,539
Life support, how is she doing?

1507
01:53:15,706 --> 01:53:18,960
We still have data. Heart and respiration
are high but within limits.

1508
01:53:19,126 --> 01:53:20,211
Ellie, do you copy?

1509
01:53:20,419 --> 01:53:23,422
If you could hear, I am okay to go.

1510
01:53:27,218 --> 01:53:29,011
<i>If you could hear me...</i>

1511
01:53:32,098 --> 01:53:33,474
Eighty percent.

1512
01:53:33,641 --> 01:53:36,477
All vibration and G-levels are in the green.

1513
01:53:41,899 --> 01:53:44,235
Control, if you're reading me...

1514
01:53:44,402 --> 01:53:46,737
...I am okay to go. Okay to go,

1515
01:53:46,904 --> 01:53:50,700
GERRY: Eighty-five percent.
MAN: All bearing tips are on the profile.

1516
01:53:51,575 --> 01:53:54,662
Ninety percent. We're detecting
some structural instability.

1517
01:53:54,829 --> 01:53:58,749
- Confirmed. Is she holding?
- We're within limits, barely.

1518
01:54:00,251 --> 01:54:02,253
I'm okay to go.

1519
01:54:03,754 --> 01:54:06,173
I'm okay to go.

1520
01:54:12,096 --> 01:54:13,556
My God!

1521
01:54:24,608 --> 01:54:27,111
Okay to go. I'm okay.

1522
01:54:27,528 --> 01:54:29,030
Hundred percent target velocity.

1523
01:54:29,196 --> 01:54:30,406
MAN:
Steve?

1524
01:54:30,573 --> 01:54:34,952
Tremendous EMI levels around the Machine.
All field measurements are off-scale.

1525
01:54:35,119 --> 01:54:37,580
Steve, we're approaching our abort limits.

1526
01:54:39,957 --> 01:54:41,042
Okay to go.

1527
01:54:46,255 --> 01:54:47,465
I hear her!

1528
01:54:47,631 --> 01:54:50,468
I hear her. Barely, but she's there.

1529
01:54:50,634 --> 01:54:53,637
- Okay to go.
- She says she's okay to go.

1530
01:54:56,640 --> 01:54:58,768
Steve, we're real close.

1531
01:54:58,934 --> 01:55:01,812
-Electrical, reading in the core?
MAN: Internal environment normal.

1532
01:55:01,979 --> 01:55:04,982
Inside the core, the weather's beautiful.

1533
01:55:09,070 --> 01:55:10,321
I'm okay to go.

1534
01:55:10,821 --> 01:55:14,283
Okay to go. I'm okay to go.

1535
01:55:14,867 --> 01:55:17,203
STEVE:
<i>Initiate drop sequence.</i>

1536
01:55:19,789 --> 01:55:21,499
On my mark.

1537
01:55:22,208 --> 01:55:24,752
Ten, nine...

1538
01:55:25,836 --> 01:55:27,838
<i>...eight...</i>

1539
01:55:28,172 --> 01:55:31,175
<i>...seven, six...</i>

1540
01:55:31,342 --> 01:55:33,969
...five, four...

1541
01:55:34,136 --> 01:55:36,806
...three, two...

1542
01:55:36,972 --> 01:55:38,349
I'm okay to go.

1543
01:55:42,311 --> 01:55:44,105
Oh, God!

1544
01:55:53,364 --> 01:55:55,032
Ellie to Control.

1545
01:55:55,699 --> 01:55:58,619
Ellie to Control, do you read me?

1546
01:56:03,040 --> 01:56:05,751
Ellie to Control, do you read me?

1547
01:56:11,507 --> 01:56:14,927
I'm gonna try to keep recording.

1548
01:56:15,678 --> 01:56:20,224
I'm going through
some kind of a tunnel.

1549
01:56:34,905 --> 01:56:36,448
There's a light source ahead.

1550
01:56:36,949 --> 01:56:40,161
Brilliant and blue-white.

1551
01:56:41,370 --> 01:56:42,997
Residual radiation?

1552
01:56:54,758 --> 01:56:56,051
It's a star.

1553
01:56:56,635 --> 01:57:00,055
I must have gone through a wormhole.

1554
01:57:05,102 --> 01:57:06,520
It's Vega.

1555
01:57:17,740 --> 01:57:19,283
I'm in another wormhole now.

1556
01:57:22,953 --> 01:57:24,079
<i>I had no idea.</i>

1557
01:57:24,246 --> 01:57:26,040
A series of them.

1558
01:57:29,043 --> 01:57:30,294
<i>They're alive.</i>

1559
01:57:30,461 --> 01:57:34,423
It's like some kind of a transit system.

1560
01:57:35,424 --> 01:57:36,800
It's a subway.

1561
01:57:36,967 --> 01:57:38,469
[YELLS]

1562
01:57:42,139 --> 01:57:43,933
Okay.

1563
01:57:44,099 --> 01:57:46,393
Okay, I've gotta keep talking.

1564
01:57:48,938 --> 01:57:52,816
It's a triple... No, quadruple system.

1565
01:57:53,234 --> 01:57:54,485
It's beautiful.

1566
01:57:57,821 --> 01:57:59,240
They're alive.

1567
01:58:07,414 --> 01:58:09,083
Another wormhole!

1568
01:58:09,416 --> 01:58:11,252
This one is much more violent!

1569
01:59:44,678 --> 01:59:46,347
Some...

1570
01:59:46,805 --> 01:59:49,141
...celestial event...

1571
01:59:50,392 --> 01:59:52,102
No...

1572
01:59:52,269 --> 01:59:55,939
No words. No words...

1573
01:59:57,608 --> 01:59:59,818
...to describe it.

1574
02:00:01,236 --> 02:00:03,113
Poetry.

1575
02:00:04,281 --> 02:00:07,785
They should have sent a poet.

1576
02:00:08,077 --> 02:00:10,662
It's so beautiful.

1577
02:00:10,829 --> 02:00:12,289
Beautiful.

1578
02:00:14,124 --> 02:00:15,959
So beautiful.

1579
02:00:17,002 --> 02:00:19,421
So beautiful.

1580
02:00:20,297 --> 02:00:23,801
I had no idea.

1581
02:00:23,967 --> 02:00:26,887
I had no idea.

1582
02:00:28,138 --> 02:00:31,475
I had no...

1583
02:00:32,768 --> 02:00:36,063
<i>I had no idea.</i>

1584
02:00:37,398 --> 02:00:44,405
<i>I had no idea. I had no idea.</i>

1585
02:03:02,376 --> 02:03:03,752
Hi, Sparks.

1586
02:03:05,629 --> 02:03:07,130
Dad?

1587
02:03:14,596 --> 02:03:16,473
I missed you.

1588
02:03:22,104 --> 02:03:23,272
Aren't you...?

1589
02:03:23,480 --> 02:03:26,692
I'm sorry I couldn't
be there for you, sweetheart.

1590
02:03:36,493 --> 02:03:38,161
You're not real.

1591
02:03:39,663 --> 02:03:41,164
None of this is real.

1592
02:03:41,331 --> 02:03:43,500
That's my scientist.

1593
02:03:44,585 --> 02:03:48,964
When I was unconscious, you downloaded
my thoughts, my memory even.

1594
02:03:49,798 --> 02:03:51,842
Pensacola.

1595
02:03:54,344 --> 02:03:57,306
We thought this might
make things easier for you.

1596
02:04:10,819 --> 02:04:14,448
- Why did you contact us?
- You contacted us.

1597
02:04:14,615 --> 02:04:16,283
We were just listening.

1598
02:04:17,159 --> 02:04:18,452
And there are others?

1599
02:04:18,619 --> 02:04:20,245
Many others.

1600
02:04:25,083 --> 02:04:26,585
They all...

1601
02:04:26,752 --> 02:04:29,880
...travel here through that
transit system that you built?

1602
02:04:30,422 --> 02:04:31,923
We didn't build it.

1603
02:04:33,091 --> 02:04:34,593
We don't know who did.

1604
02:04:36,678 --> 02:04:39,640
No, they were gone
long before we ever got here.

1605
02:04:40,265 --> 02:04:42,100
Maybe someday they'll come back.

1606
02:04:42,934 --> 02:04:45,646
All the other civilizations that you find,
they come here?

1607
02:04:45,854 --> 02:04:47,064
Not all.

1608
02:04:48,857 --> 02:04:52,027
- Is this some test?
- No, no tests.

1609
02:04:54,946 --> 02:04:56,531
You have your mother's hands.

1610
02:05:06,708 --> 02:05:08,877
You're an interesting species...

1611
02:05:09,544 --> 02:05:11,505
...an interesting mix.

1612
02:05:12,422 --> 02:05:15,217
You're capable of
such beautiful dreams...

1613
02:05:16,259 --> 02:05:18,720
...and such horrible nightmares.

1614
02:05:19,888 --> 02:05:21,723
You feel so lost...

1615
02:05:22,307 --> 02:05:25,268
...so cut off, so alone.

1616
02:05:26,728 --> 02:05:28,480
Only you're not.

1617
02:05:30,565 --> 02:05:32,109
See...

1618
02:05:32,567 --> 02:05:34,194
In all our searching...

1619
02:05:34,403 --> 02:05:37,906
...the only thing we've found
that makes the emptiness bearable...

1620
02:05:38,073 --> 02:05:39,700
...is each other.

1621
02:05:43,453 --> 02:05:45,372
What happens now?

1622
02:05:46,415 --> 02:05:47,916
Now?

1623
02:05:48,834 --> 02:05:50,627
You go home.

1624
02:05:50,794 --> 02:05:52,212
ELLIE:
Home?

1625
02:05:52,379 --> 02:05:55,465
But I have so many questions.

1626
02:05:55,632 --> 02:05:57,759
Do we get to come back?

1627
02:05:58,635 --> 02:06:00,887
This was just a first step.

1628
02:06:01,054 --> 02:06:02,806
In time, you'll take another.

1629
02:06:04,391 --> 02:06:06,768
But other people need to see
what I've seen...

1630
02:06:06,935 --> 02:06:09,980
This is the way it's been done
for billions of years.

1631
02:06:11,982 --> 02:06:14,609
- But I...
- Small moves, Ellie.

1632
02:06:16,069 --> 02:06:17,988
Small moves.

1633
02:07:01,072 --> 02:07:02,866
We've got video on Ellie.
Five-by-five.

1634
02:07:03,074 --> 02:07:04,951
STEVE:
She's through! She is in the net.

1635
02:07:05,118 --> 02:07:08,121
She's alive. Get the White House.
Get the president on the phone.

1636
02:07:08,288 --> 02:07:10,665
What's going on, Steve? How is she?
How is she?

1637
02:07:10,832 --> 02:07:12,542
STEVE:
Ellie, this is Control. Do you copy?

1638
02:07:12,709 --> 02:07:14,753
KENT: What's going on?
- I don't know. Hang on.

1639
02:07:14,920 --> 02:07:16,546
-She is alive.
STEVE: Put her on the PA.

1640
02:07:16,713 --> 02:07:18,298
Ellie, just hold on tight.

1641
02:07:18,924 --> 02:07:22,219
We're still trying to determine
the nature of the malfunction...

1642
02:07:22,385 --> 02:07:24,888
...but the important thing is you're okay.

1643
02:07:25,055 --> 02:07:26,223
<i>What?</i>

1644
02:07:26,389 --> 02:07:30,185
It's all right, Ellie.
The important thing is that you're safe.

1645
02:07:31,144 --> 02:07:33,438
<i>What malfunction?</i>

1646
02:07:34,773 --> 02:07:36,233
<i>What...?</i>

1647
02:07:37,400 --> 02:07:39,027
<i>What happened?</i>

1648
02:07:40,362 --> 02:07:42,030
<i>What day is this?</i>

1649
02:07:42,697 --> 02:07:45,700
I'm sorry, Ellie,
did you say what day?

1650
02:07:46,785 --> 02:07:49,037
<i>How long was I gone?</i>

1651
02:07:50,789 --> 02:07:55,585
Ellie, the IPV dropped
straight through the Machine.

1652
02:07:55,752 --> 02:07:57,420
You didn't go anywhere.

1653
02:07:58,296 --> 02:08:01,633
But I did. I did go.

1654
02:08:01,800 --> 02:08:04,177
That's what I've been trying
to tell everyone.

1655
02:08:08,640 --> 02:08:11,726
But the headset
didn't record a single image?

1656
02:08:11,893 --> 02:08:14,521
Just this. Static.

1657
02:08:14,729 --> 02:08:15,772
Thank you.

1658
02:08:15,939 --> 02:08:19,359
Now, doctor, let me show you
what we saw.

1659
02:08:28,827 --> 02:08:32,664
Forty-three separate remote cameras
show exactly the same event.

1660
02:08:32,831 --> 02:08:34,374
The IPV dropped straight through.

1661
02:08:34,541 --> 02:08:38,128
Everyone who watched
saw exactly the same thing.

1662
02:08:38,295 --> 02:08:40,130
Nothing happened.

1663
02:08:41,464 --> 02:08:43,758
How do you explain this, doctor?

1664
02:08:45,760 --> 02:08:46,887
I can't.

1665
02:08:47,095 --> 02:08:48,555
CLINTON:
<i>I would encourage you...</i>

1666
02:08:50,348 --> 02:08:54,519
<i>...not to inflame this situation
beyond the facts.</i>

1667
02:08:54,686 --> 02:08:58,106
<i>Let us deal with this on the facts.</i>

1668
02:08:58,273 --> 02:09:01,151
<i>We are monitoring
what has actually happened.</i>

1669
02:09:01,318 --> 02:09:06,656
<i>We are taking what we believe
is factually appropriate steps.</i>

1670
02:09:06,823 --> 02:09:10,035
<i>Amid a flurry of accusations
and counter-accusations...</i>

1671
02:09:10,243 --> 02:09:12,829
<i>...by the nations representing
the Machine Consortium...</i>

1672
02:09:12,996 --> 02:09:15,916
<i>...the president has ordered
a special inquiry.</i>

1673
02:09:16,124 --> 02:09:18,919
<i>However, the appointment
to the committee of Michael Kitz...</i>

1674
02:09:19,127 --> 02:09:21,963
<i>...who has recently resigned
as National Security Advisor...</i>

1675
02:09:22,130 --> 02:09:24,466
<i>...has raised a number of eyebrows
on Capitol Hill.</i>

1676
02:09:24,633 --> 02:09:28,428
<i>I have made no decision to run for
Congress, I have announced no candidacy.</i>

1677
02:09:28,595 --> 02:09:31,181
<i>The fact that I resigned
as National Security Advisor...</i>

1678
02:09:31,348 --> 02:09:33,058
<i>...has nothing to do with anything...</i>

1679
02:09:33,224 --> 02:09:36,394
<i>...other than I want to devote
all of my energy to this inquiry.</i>

1680
02:09:37,145 --> 02:09:41,816
<i>The people of the world have a right
to know what really happened.</i>

1681
02:09:42,651 --> 02:09:45,153
And yet every scientific
instrument confirms...

1682
02:09:45,320 --> 02:09:49,491
...the IPV was out of contact
for only a fraction of a second.

1683
02:09:49,658 --> 02:09:51,701
Isn't that correct, doctor?

1684
02:09:53,411 --> 02:09:55,705
A fraction of a second Earth time, yes.

1685
02:09:56,581 --> 02:09:57,832
SENATOR:
Earth time?

1686
02:09:57,999 --> 02:10:01,628
ELLIE: Senator, I believe that the Machine
opened up a wormhole...

1687
02:10:01,795 --> 02:10:06,758
...a tunnel through the fabric of space-time,
also known as an Einstein-Rosen Bridge.

1688
02:10:06,925 --> 02:10:11,054
Now, because of the effects of general
relativity, what I experienced as 18 hours...

1689
02:10:11,221 --> 02:10:13,473
...passed instantaneously on Earth.

1690
02:10:13,640 --> 02:10:17,978
Isn't it true that these wormholes you
speak of are merely theoretical predictions?

1691
02:10:18,144 --> 02:10:20,897
There is no evidence
they actually exist, is there?

1692
02:10:21,064 --> 02:10:22,774
ELLIE:
There is no direct evidence, no.

1693
02:10:22,941 --> 02:10:24,693
KITZ:
Tell me something, doctor...

1694
02:10:24,859 --> 02:10:27,988
...why do you think these aliens
would go to all this trouble?

1695
02:10:28,154 --> 02:10:32,367
Bring you tens of thousands of light years
and then send you home without proof?

1696
02:10:33,034 --> 02:10:35,745
They said that's how it's been done
for billions of years.

1697
02:10:36,121 --> 02:10:39,708
KITZ: <i>That's very neat. You have no proof
because they didn't want you to have any.</i>

1698
02:10:39,874 --> 02:10:44,629
<i>A phenomenon known in psychiatric circles,
I believe, as a self-reinforcing delusion.</i>

1699
02:10:45,213 --> 02:10:46,840
<i>Is that what you think? Delusional?</i>

1700
02:10:47,007 --> 02:10:51,136
KITZ: <i>Well, I do think you may have suffered
some kind of an episode. Yeah, I do.</i>

1701
02:10:51,302 --> 02:10:54,180
<i>Doctor, I'd like to propose
an alternate hypothesis...</i>

1702
02:10:54,389 --> 02:10:58,059
<i>...and I'd like you to bring
your scientific expertise to bear on it.</i>

1703
02:10:58,226 --> 02:11:01,438
To fake a signal from Vega...

1704
02:11:02,105 --> 02:11:03,690
...what would you need?

1705
02:11:05,400 --> 02:11:09,195
You would need a satellite to transmit
the signal, but it would be impossible to...

1706
02:11:09,362 --> 02:11:13,283
You'd need a satellite and need launch
capabilities to put the satellite into orbit.

1707
02:11:13,450 --> 02:11:16,661
And the message itself.
To put something like this together...

1708
02:11:16,828 --> 02:11:19,122
...so complex,
drawing on many disciplines...

1709
02:11:19,289 --> 02:11:21,249
-Would be impossible.
KITZ: Impossible?

1710
02:11:22,542 --> 02:11:24,461
Impossible?

1711
02:11:24,627 --> 02:11:28,256
Is there anyone who might
have been up to the challenge?

1712
02:11:28,423 --> 02:11:30,884
Someone with extraordinary
technical expertise...

1713
02:11:31,051 --> 02:11:33,261
...enormous financial resources...

1714
02:11:33,428 --> 02:11:37,807
...someone perverse enough,
eccentric enough to come up with the idea?

1715
02:11:39,184 --> 02:11:40,226
Hadden?

1716
02:11:41,186 --> 02:11:43,646
S. R. Hadden.

1717
02:11:43,813 --> 02:11:46,441
[CROWD MURMURING]

1718
02:11:47,942 --> 02:11:50,987
You're implying that this was
all some kind of a hoax?

1719
02:11:51,154 --> 02:11:52,405
That he engineered this?

1720
02:11:52,572 --> 02:11:53,823
KITZ:
S. R. Hadden...

1721
02:11:53,990 --> 02:11:58,870
<i>...a legendary power broker and manipulator
in perhaps his final bid for immortality.</i>

1722
02:11:59,037 --> 02:12:01,706
<i>Maybe he wanted to explore
and exploit new technologies...</i>

1723
02:12:01,873 --> 02:12:04,084
<i>...and get the governments
to pick up the tab.</i>

1724
02:12:04,292 --> 02:12:07,045
<i>Perhaps this was his
final altruistic gesture...</i>

1725
02:12:07,295 --> 02:12:11,007
<i>...to unite the world in some common goal.
Maybe all of the above.</i>

1726
02:12:11,174 --> 02:12:16,096
<i>S. R. Hadden, a brilliant
and complicated man.</i>

1727
02:12:18,640 --> 02:12:22,894
Doctor, are you familiar
with the scientific precept...

1728
02:12:23,061 --> 02:12:25,188
...known as Occam's razor?

1729
02:12:31,402 --> 02:12:32,570
ELLIE:
Yes.

1730
02:12:34,572 --> 02:12:36,658
It means that all things being equal...

1731
02:12:36,825 --> 02:12:39,327
...the simplest explanation
tends to be the right one.

1732
02:12:39,494 --> 02:12:41,037
KITZ:
Exactly.

1733
02:12:41,204 --> 02:12:43,665
Now, you tell me.
What is more likely here?

1734
02:12:43,832 --> 02:12:47,293
That a message from aliens
results in a magical machine...

1735
02:12:47,460 --> 02:12:50,171
...that whisks you away
to the center of the galaxy...

1736
02:12:50,380 --> 02:12:53,258
...to go windsurfing with your dad
and a split second later...

1737
02:12:53,424 --> 02:12:56,886
...returns you home
without a single shred of proof?

1738
02:12:57,053 --> 02:13:00,849
Or that your experience
is the result of being...

1739
02:13:01,015 --> 02:13:05,478
...the unwitting star in the farewell
performance of one S.R. Hadden?

1740
02:13:05,645 --> 02:13:09,440
A man with the means, motive and
opportunity to play you and the rest of us...

1741
02:13:09,691 --> 02:13:13,903
...as pawns in the biggest, most elaborate,
most expensive hoax of all time?

1742
02:13:15,196 --> 02:13:17,115
MAN:
Dr. Arroway.

1743
02:13:18,992 --> 02:13:23,371
You come to us with no evidence,
no record...

1744
02:13:23,538 --> 02:13:25,331
...no artifacts.

1745
02:13:25,498 --> 02:13:29,669
Only a story that, to put it mildly,
strains credibility.

1746
02:13:30,795 --> 02:13:33,923
Over half a trillion dollars were spent.

1747
02:13:34,090 --> 02:13:37,093
Dozens of lives were lost.

1748
02:13:37,719 --> 02:13:42,432
Are you really gonna sit there
and tell us we should just take this all...

1749
02:13:43,183 --> 02:13:44,726
...on faith?

1750
02:13:48,730 --> 02:13:50,940
KITZ:
Please answer the question, doctor.

1751
02:13:54,861 --> 02:13:56,946
Is it possible that it didn't happen?

1752
02:13:57,113 --> 02:13:58,281
Yes.

1753
02:13:58,448 --> 02:14:00,992
[CROWD MURMURING]

1754
02:14:01,159 --> 02:14:04,287
As a scientist I must concede that.
I must volunteer that.

1755
02:14:08,625 --> 02:14:10,543
KITZ:
Wait a minute, let me get this straight.

1756
02:14:10,710 --> 02:14:14,589
You admit you have absolutely no
physical evidence to back up your story?

1757
02:14:14,923 --> 02:14:15,965
Yes.

1758
02:14:16,132 --> 02:14:19,177
You admit that you very well may have
hallucinated this whole thing?

1759
02:14:19,344 --> 02:14:22,138
- Yes.
- You admit that if you were in our position...

1760
02:14:22,305 --> 02:14:26,059
...you would respond with exactly the same
degree of incredulity and skepticism?

1761
02:14:26,226 --> 02:14:27,477
Yes.

1762
02:14:27,644 --> 02:14:30,605
Then why don't you simply withdraw
your testimony and concede...

1763
02:14:30,772 --> 02:14:35,944
...that this journey to the center of
the galaxy in fact never took place?

1764
02:14:38,947 --> 02:14:40,949
Because I can't.

1765
02:14:41,115 --> 02:14:43,826
[CROWD MURMURING]

1766
02:14:47,497 --> 02:14:51,167
I had an experience.

1767
02:14:52,835 --> 02:14:55,421
I can't prove it. I can't even explain it.

1768
02:14:55,588 --> 02:14:58,675
But everything that I know as
a human being, everything I am...

1769
02:14:58,841 --> 02:15:00,969
...tells me it was real.

1770
02:15:03,012 --> 02:15:06,891
I was given something wonderful,
something that changed me forever.

1771
02:15:07,058 --> 02:15:08,601
A vision...

1772
02:15:08,768 --> 02:15:10,853
...of the universe...

1773
02:15:11,020 --> 02:15:13,690
...that tells us undeniably...

1774
02:15:13,856 --> 02:15:16,109
...how tiny and insignificant...

1775
02:15:16,276 --> 02:15:19,988
...and how rare and precious we all are.

1776
02:15:21,155 --> 02:15:23,574
A vision that tells us
that we belong to something...

1777
02:15:23,741 --> 02:15:29,414
...that is greater than ourselves,
that we are not... That none of us are alone.

1778
02:15:34,043 --> 02:15:38,047
I wish I could share that.

1779
02:15:40,550 --> 02:15:41,718
I wish...

1780
02:15:43,177 --> 02:15:47,223
...that everyone,
if even for one moment...

1781
02:15:47,390 --> 02:15:53,146
...could feel that awe
and humility and hope.

1782
02:15:53,563 --> 02:15:55,565
But...

1783
02:16:01,738 --> 02:16:04,032
That continues to be my wish.

1784
02:16:45,198 --> 02:16:48,534
MAN 1: Dr. Arroway, get a picture, please?
Get a photo over here?

1785
02:16:50,161 --> 02:16:51,788
WOMAN:
Here comes Ellie.

1786
02:17:28,074 --> 02:17:30,993
MAN 2:
Reverend Joss! Reverend Joss!

1787
02:17:31,160 --> 02:17:34,539
MAN 3: Reverend Joss, what do you
believe? What do you believe?

1788
02:17:38,793 --> 02:17:42,797
As a person of faith, I'm bound by
a different covenant than Dr. Arroway.

1789
02:17:44,549 --> 02:17:46,801
But our goal is one and the same.

1790
02:17:47,301 --> 02:17:48,928
The pursuit of truth.

1791
02:17:52,473 --> 02:17:53,766
I, for one, believe her.

1792
02:18:17,331 --> 02:18:20,209
<i>So, what happens now?
We give her a medal?</i>

1793
02:18:20,376 --> 02:18:23,254
I'd say at least a healthy grant.

1794
02:18:23,421 --> 02:18:26,883
I assume you read the confidential report
from the investigating committee?

1795
02:18:27,091 --> 02:18:28,843
KITZ:
<i>I flipped through it.</i>

1796
02:18:29,010 --> 02:18:33,222
I was especially interested
in the section on Arroway's video unit.

1797
02:18:34,015 --> 02:18:36,350
The one that recorded the static.

1798
02:18:37,602 --> 02:18:39,312
<i>Continue.</i>

1799
02:18:39,937 --> 02:18:43,774
The fact that it recorded static
isn't what interests me.

1800
02:18:48,613 --> 02:18:49,989
<i>Continue.</i>

1801
02:18:50,865 --> 02:18:52,700
What interests me...

1802
02:18:52,867 --> 02:18:56,704
...is that it recorded
approximately 18 hours of it.

1803
02:19:01,959 --> 02:19:03,878
<i>That is interesting, isn't it?</i>

1804
02:19:10,551 --> 02:19:14,388
ELLIE: You can see over there,
we're building 45 brand-new dishes.

1805
02:19:14,555 --> 02:19:18,518
And that means when you put them
together with all the radio telescopes...

1806
02:19:18,684 --> 02:19:20,186
...all over the world...

1807
02:19:20,353 --> 02:19:25,024
...then we get to hear farther into space
than anybody has ever heard before.

1808
02:19:25,191 --> 02:19:26,234
Yeah.

1809
02:19:26,400 --> 02:19:29,237
Are there other people
out there in the universe?

1810
02:19:30,279 --> 02:19:33,032
That's a good question.
What do you think, huh?

1811
02:19:33,616 --> 02:19:36,953
- I don't know.
- That's a good answer.

1812
02:19:37,828 --> 02:19:38,871
Skeptic, huh?

1813
02:19:40,164 --> 02:19:45,044
The important thing is that you all
keep searching for your own answers.

1814
02:19:45,211 --> 02:19:48,047
I'll tell you one thing
about the universe, though.

1815
02:19:48,214 --> 02:19:52,093
The universe is a pretty big place.

1816
02:19:53,094 --> 02:19:58,599
It's bigger than anything
anyone has ever dreamed of before.

1817
02:20:00,184 --> 02:20:01,852
So if it's just us...

1818
02:20:03,479 --> 02:20:06,274
...it seems like an awful waste of space.

1819
02:20:07,775 --> 02:20:09,151
Right?

